OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026", "title": "The strong thin tree conjecture", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "174", "family_title": "Deterministic construction of strong thin spanning trees", "abstract_html": "We prove that every finite loopless <i>k</i>-edge-connected multigraph on at least two vertices, with <i>k</i> ≥ 1, has a spanning tree meeting each cut in at most \\(C/k\\) times the size of the cut, where <i>C</i> is a universal constant. This resolves the strong thin tree conjecture.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Strong thin tree theorem — paper Theorem 1.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Strong thin tree theorem — paper Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G=(V,E)\\) is a finite undirected multigraph without loops, with \\(|V|\\ge 2\\). Parallel edges are counted separately.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V\\), \\(\\delta_G(S)\\) is the set of edges with exactly one endpoint in \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  |\\delta_G(S)|\\ge k\\qquad(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A spanning tree \\(T\\) is \\(\\alpha\\)-thin if\n  \\[\n  |\\delta_T(S)|\\le \\alpha|\\delta_G(S)|\n  \\qquad(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a universal constant \\(C&gt;0\\) such that every such graph has a spanning tree \\(T\\) satisfying\n\\[\n|\\delta_T(S)|\\le \\frac Ck|\\delta_G(S)|\n\\qquad(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an absolute integer \\(r_*\\ge r_{\\mathrm{step}}\\). Proposition 13 gives, for every integer \\(r\\ge r_*\\),\n\\[\n\\frac5{16}\\le\\frac{r'}r\\le\\frac12,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{1/2+3\\tau}{r'/r}\\le1+15r^{-1/16}.\n\\]</p><p>First let \\(H_0\\) have \\(r_0\\ge1\\) packed spanning trees. While \\(r_i\\ge r_*\\), apply Proposition 13 to obtain a spanning subgraph \\(H_{i+1}\\) with\n\\[\nr_{i+1}=\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{(1-4r_i^{-1/16})r_i}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\npacked trees. Stop at the first \\(j\\) with \\(r_j&lt;r_*\\). Every \\(r_i\\) is positive, and termination follows from \\(r_{i+1}\\le r_i/2\\).</p><p>For \\(j\\ge1\\), reading this inequality backward gives\n\\[\nr_i\\ge2^{j-1-i}r_{j-1}\\ge2^{j-1-i}r_*\n\\qquad(0\\le i&lt;j).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1}r_i^{-1/16}\n\\le\\frac{r_*^{-1/16}}{1-2^{-1/16}}.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{it}}\n=\n\\exp\\left(\\frac{15r_*^{-1/16}}{1-2^{-1/16}}\\right).\n\\]\nMultiplying the cut inequalities and relative-loss estimates gives, simultaneously for all nonempty proper \\(S\\),\n\\[\n|\\delta_{H_j}(S)|\n\\le\n\\frac{r_j}{r_0}\n\\prod_{i=0}^{j-1}(1+15r_i^{-1/16})\n|\\delta_{H_0}(S)|\n\\le\nC_{\\mathrm{it}}\\frac{r_j}{r_0}|\\delta_{H_0}(S)|.\n\\]\nFor \\(j=0\\), the same inequality holds with an empty product.</p><p>Since \\(r_j\\ge1\\), \\(H_j\\) is connected. Any spanning tree \\(T\\subseteq H_j\\) therefore satisfies\n\\[\n|\\delta_T(S)|\n\\le\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{it}}r_*}{r_0}|\\delta_{H_0}(S)|.\n\\]\nThis also covers \\(r_0&lt;r_*\\): there are no rounds, and the displayed factor is at least one.</p><p>Now take \\(H_0=G\\). For \\(k\\ge2\\), summing the cut lower bounds over the \\(d\\ge2\\) parts of a partition gives\n\\[\n|E_G(\\mathcal P)|\\ge\\frac{kd}{2}\n\\ge\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor(d-1).\n\\]\nProposition 1 supplies \\(\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\) packed trees. For \\(k=1\\), connectedness supplies one tree. Thus\n\\[\nr_0=\\max\\{1,\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\}\\ge k/3.\n\\]\nSubstitution proves the theorem with\n\\[\nC=3C_{\\mathrm{it}}r_*,\n\\]\na universal constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Laplacian and tree-packing tools — paper Lemmas 2.1, 2.2, 2.4 and Theorem 2.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Laplacian and tree-packing tools — paper Lemmas 2.1, 2.2, 2.4 and Theorem 2.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All graphs are finite undirected loopless multigraphs. Every parallel edge has its own index.</p><p class=\"li\">An arbitrary orientation of \\(e=uv\\) defines\n  \\[\n  b_e=\\mathbf1_u-\\mathbf1_v,\\qquad\n  L_H=\\sum_{e\\in E(H)}b_eb_e^{\\mathsf T}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(X\\succ0\\), write\n  \\[\n  R_X(e)=b_e^{\\mathsf T}X^{-1}b_e.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\preceq B\\) means that \\(B-A\\) is positive semidefinite.</p><p class=\"li\">For a partition \\(\\mathcal P\\) of the vertex set, \\(E_H(\\mathcal P)\\) denotes the edges joining distinct parts.</p><p class=\"li\">A graph is \\(r\\)-tree-packable if it contains \\(r\\) pairwise edge-disjoint spanning trees. A singleton vertex set is taken to be \\(r\\)-tree-packable for every positive integer \\(r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following tools hold:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Rank-one semidefinite comparison is equivalent to a resistance bound, and resistance decreases when the positive definite matrix increases.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Incidence rank is the number of vertices minus the number of connected components.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(r\\)-tree-packability is equivalent to the partition inequalities\n   \\[\n   |E_H(\\mathcal P)|\\ge r(|\\mathcal P|-1).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Inclusion-maximal \\(q\\)-tree-packable induced vertex sets partition the vertices, coarsen when edges are added, and, unless there is only one part, have fewer than \\(q(|\\mathcal P|-1)\\) crossing edges.</p>", "proof": "<p>The four assertions are Lemmas 1.1–1.4. In addition, the Laplacian definition gives\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}L_H\\mathbf1_S=|\\delta_H(S)|.\n\\]\nChanging an edge orientation changes none of the matrices or resistances used here.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\succ0\\) is a real symmetric matrix, \\(b\\) is a real column of the same dimension, and \\(t&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nb^{\\mathsf T}X^{-1}b\\le t\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nbb^{\\mathsf T}\\preceq tX.\n\\]\nIf \\(X\\succeq Y\\succ0\\), then\n\\[\nb^{\\mathsf T}X^{-1}b\\le b^{\\mathsf T}Y^{-1}b.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Congruence by \\(X^{-1/2}\\) reduces the first assertion to the fact that the only possible nonzero eigenvalue of \\(aa^{\\mathsf T}\\) is \\(\\|a\\|^2\\), with \\(a=X^{-1/2}b\\). For the second,\n\\[\nY^{-1/2}XY^{-1/2}\\succeq I;\n\\]\ndiagonalization and inversion give \\(X^{-1}\\preceq Y^{-1}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Incidence rank — paper Lemma 2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Incidence rank — paper Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(F\\) has \\(n\\) vertices and \\(c\\) connected components.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Its incidence columns span a space of dimension \\(n-c\\). In particular, the incidence columns of a spanning tree form a basis of\n\\[\n\\mathbf1^\\perp\n=\n\\left\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^V:\\sum_{v\\in V}x_v=0\\right\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A vector is orthogonal to every incidence column precisely when its coordinates agree at the endpoints of each edge, equivalently when it is constant on every connected component. The orthogonal complement thus has dimension \\(c\\). A tree on \\(n\\) vertices has \\(n-1\\) edges, as follows by deleting leaves successively, so its spanning columns form a basis.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Nash–Williams–Tutte partition criterion — paper Theorem 2.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Nash–Williams–Tutte partition criterion — paper Theorem 2.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(H\\) is a finite loopless multigraph.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(r\\) is a positive integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(H\\) contains \\(r\\) edge-disjoint spanning trees if and only if\n\\[\n|E_H(\\mathcal P)|\\ge r(|\\mathcal P|-1)\n\\]\nfor every partition \\(\\mathcal P\\) of \\(V(H)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Necessity follows by counting the crossings of each tree.</p><p>For sufficiency, choose \\(r\\) disjoint forests whose total number \\(m\\) of edges is maximum. A state is such a choice. Starting from this state, allow a move that adds an unused edge to one forest and removes an edge of the resulting cycle.</p><p>In every reachable state, every unused edge closes a cycle with each forest: otherwise adding it would increase \\(m\\). Let \\(U\\) be the set of edges that are unused in at least one reachable state. If an unused edge is added to a forest, every edge of the resulting cycle lies in \\(U\\), since any one of its original edges can be made unused by the corresponding move. Such a move preserves the components of that forest restricted to its edges in \\(U\\).</p><p>Fix \\(e\\in U\\). In a state where \\(e\\) is unused, its endpoints are connected by edges in \\(U\\) in each forest. Component invariance carries this statement to the initial state. Consequently each component of \\((V(H),U)\\) is spanned by the \\(U\\)-edges of every forest.</p><p>If there are \\(d\\) such components and \\(n=|V(H)|\\), this accounts for \\(r(n-d)\\) used edges. Every edge between these components is used, since every unused edge belongs to \\(U\\). Their number is therefore at most\n\\[\nm-r(n-d).\n\\]\nIf \\(m&lt;r(n-1)\\), this is smaller than \\(r(d-1)\\), contrary to the partition hypothesis. Thus \\(m=r(n-1)\\); each forest has \\(n-1\\) edges and is a spanning tree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Union and quotient properties — paper Lemma 2.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Union and quotient properties — paper Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(q\\) is a positive integer and \\(F\\) is a finite loopless multigraph.</p><p class=\"li\">A vertex set is called \\(q\\)-tree-packable when its induced subgraph is \\(q\\)-tree-packable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(F[A]\\) and \\(F[B]\\) are \\(q\\)-tree-packable and \\(A\\cap B\\ne\\varnothing\\), then \\(F[A\\cup B]\\) is \\(q\\)-tree-packable.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The inclusion-maximal \\(q\\)-tree-packable vertex sets form a partition \\(\\mathcal P(F)\\). If \\(F\\subseteq F'\\), then \\(\\mathcal P(F')\\) coarsens \\(\\mathcal P(F)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(|\\mathcal P(F)|&gt;1\\), then\n   \\[\n   |E_F(\\mathcal P(F))|&lt;q(|\\mathcal P(F)|-1).\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, enumerate the \\(q\\) trees on each of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). In the \\(i\\)-th tree on \\(B\\), contract all vertices of \\(A\\cap B\\) to one vertex, discard loops, and choose a spanning tree of the resulting connected multigraph. Its selected edges, together with the \\(i\\)-th tree on \\(A\\), form a tree on \\(A\\cup B\\). Every selected edge has an endpoint outside \\(A\\), and selections in different colors remain disjoint.</p><p>Every singleton is packable, and finiteness gives maximal sets. The first assertion shows that two maximal sets cannot intersect unless equal. Previously packable sets remain packable when edges are added, proving the second assertion.</p><p>For the third, contract the parts and remove loops. If the quotient crossing count were at least \\(q(d-1)\\), where \\(d=|\\mathcal P(F)|&gt;1\\), there would be a set \\(W\\) of at least two quotient vertices with\n\\[\n|E(W)|\\ge q(|W|-1).\n\\]\nChoose \\(W\\) inclusion-minimal. Every proper nonempty \\(J\\subset W\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|E(J)|\\le q(|J|-1).\n\\]\nFor a partition \\(W=J_1\\sqcup\\cdots\\sqcup J_t\\), subtraction gives at least\n\\[\nq(|W|-1)-\\sum_{\\ell=1}^tq(|J_\\ell|-1)=q(t-1)\n\\]\ncrossing edges. Lemma 1.3 supplies \\(q\\) quotient trees on \\(W\\). Attach, in each color, a packed tree inside each quotient vertex. This gives \\(q\\) disjoint spanning trees on the union of the corresponding parts, contradicting maximality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Dual reduction for a hierarchy — paper Lemma B.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Dual reduction for a hierarchy — paper Lemma B.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G=(V,E)\\) is connected, loopless, with \\(n=|V|\\ge2\\), \\(m=|E|\\), incidence matrix \\(B=[b_e]_{e\\in E}\\), and \\(L_G=BB^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C=\\{S:\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V\\},\n  \\qquad d_S=|\\delta_G(S)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A hierarchy is a finite rooted tree of distinct nonempty vertex sets, with root \\(V\\), singleton leaves, and children partitioning each nonleaf into at least two sets.</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonroot node \\(t\\), write \\(V(t)\\) for its vertex set, \\(t^*\\) for its parent, and\n  \\[\n  P(t)=\\delta_G(V(t)),\\qquad\n  O(t)=E_G(V(t),V(t^*)\\setminus V(t)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal M\\) is a collection of nonroot nodes with \\(O(t)\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  p^*=\n  \\inf_{\\substack{D\\succ0\\\\\n  \\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}D\\mathbf1_S\\le d_S\\;(S\\in\\mathcal C)}}\n  \\max_{t\\in\\mathcal M}\n  \\frac1{|O(t)|}\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}b_e^{\\mathsf T}D^{-1}b_e.\n  \\]\n  For \\(\\mathcal M=\\varnothing\\), set \\(p^*=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(X\\in\\{0,1\\}^{h\\times V}\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E(X)=\\sum_{e\\in E}\\|Xb_e\\|_2^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(U\\in\\mathbb R^{E\\times h}\\) is row semiorthogonal if \\(UU^{\\mathsf T}=I_m\\); its \\(e\\)-th row is \\(u_e^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  Q^*=\n  \\sup_{\\substack{h\\ge m,\\;X\\in\\{0,1\\}^{h\\times V}\\\\\n  \\mathcal E(X)&gt;0,\\;UU^{\\mathsf T}=I_m}}\n  \\frac{\n  \\displaystyle\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal M}\\frac1{|O(t)|}\n  \\left(\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}\\langle u_e,Xb_e\\rangle\\right)^2}\n  {\\mathcal E(X)}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\np^*\\le Q^*\\le2p^*.\n\\]\nConsequently, if every displayed ratio is at most \\(K\\), then for every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) there is a full positive definite \\(D\\) satisfying the cut constraints and every marked average-resistance bound with right side \\(K+\\eta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\mathcal M=\\varnothing\\), take a sufficiently small positive multiple of the identity. Assume otherwise.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nA_t=\\frac1{|O(t)|}\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}b_eb_e^{\\mathsf T},\n\\quad\nf_t(D)=\\operatorname{tr}(A_tD^{-1}),\n\\quad\ng_S(D)=\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}D\\mathbf1_S-d_S.\n\\]\nEach \\(f_t\\) is convex, since\n\\[\nb^{\\mathsf T}D^{-1}b\n=\n\\sup_x\\{2b^{\\mathsf T}x-x^{\\mathsf T}Dx\\}.\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\varepsilon_0&lt;\\min_{S\\in\\mathcal C}\\frac{d_S}{|S|},\n\\qquad D_0=\\varepsilon_0I.\n\\]\nAll cut constraints are strict at \\(D_0\\), and \\(0\\le p^*&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p>Consider the open convex set of \\((a,b,s)\\) for which some \\(D\\succ0\\) and \\(r\\in\\mathbb R\\) satisfy\n\\[\nf_t(D)-r&lt;a_t,\\qquad g_S(D)&lt;b_S,\\qquad r&lt;s.\n\\]\nIt excludes \\((0,0,p^*)\\). Lemma 2.1 supplies nonzero nonnegative coefficients \\((\\lambda,y,\\alpha)\\) with\n\\[\n\\lambda\\cdot a+y\\cdot b+\\alpha s\\ge\\alpha p^*.\n\\]\nNonnegativity follows because any coordinate can be increased arbitrarily. Strict feasibility at \\(D_0\\), with \\(r_0&gt;\\max_tf_t(D_0)\\), shows \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\). Normalize \\(\\alpha=1\\) and take limits to the strict inequalities. Since \\(r\\) is unrestricted, \\(\\sum_t\\lambda_t=1\\).</p><p>Weak duality then gives\n\\[\np^*=\n\\sup_{\\substack{\\lambda\\ge0,\\;\\sum_t\\lambda_t=1\\\\y\\ge0}}\n\\inf_{D\\succ0}\n\\left\\{\n\\operatorname{tr}(A(\\lambda)D^{-1})\n+\\operatorname{tr}(Z(y)D)-d(y)\n\\right\\},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nA(\\lambda)=\\sum_t\\lambda_tA_t,\\quad\nZ(y)=\\sum_{S\\in\\mathcal C}y_S\\mathbf1_S\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T},\n\\quad\nd(y)=\\sum_Sy_Sd_S.\n\\]\nThis argument does not assume attainment of the primal infimum.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nN(\\lambda,y)=\n\\operatorname{tr}\\left((Z(y)^{1/2}A(\\lambda)Z(y)^{1/2})^{1/2}\\right).\n\\]\nBy Lemma 2.2, scaling nonzero \\(y\\) by \\(s\\ge0\\) gives the dual value\n\\[\n2\\sqrt s\\,N(\\lambda,y)-sd(y),\n\\]\nwhose supremum is \\(N(\\lambda,y)^2/d(y)\\). Therefore\n\\[\np^*=\n\\sup_{\\substack{\\lambda\\ge0,\\;\\sum_t\\lambda_t=1\\\\y\\ge0,\\;y\\ne0}}\n\\frac{N(\\lambda,y)^2}{d(y)}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(W(\\lambda)\\) be diagonal, with\n\\[\nw_e=\n\\left(\\sum_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathcal M\\\\e\\in O(t)}}\n\\frac{\\lambda_t}{|O(t)|}\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThen \\(A(\\lambda)=BW^2B^{\\mathsf T}\\). If \\(Y\\) has rows \\(\\sqrt{y_S}\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}\\), then\n\\[\nN(\\lambda,y)=\\|YBW\\|_*,\n\\qquad\nd(y)=\\sum_e\\|Yb_e\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_*\\) is the sum of singular values; the identity follows because the relevant products \\(TT^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\(T^{\\mathsf T}T\\) have the same nonzero eigenvalues.</p><p>Approximate \\(y\\) by nonzero nonnegative rational vectors. For rational \\(y\\), repeat each binary cut row \\(\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}\\) an integral multiple of \\(y_S\\) times. The numerator and denominator acquire the same factor. Conversely, discard constant rows of any binary matrix; they contribute zero to \\(XB\\). Adding zero rows permits \\(h\\ge m\\). Hence\n\\[\np^*=\n\\sup_{\\substack{h\\ge m,\\;X\\in\\{0,1\\}^{h\\times V},\\;\\mathcal E(X)&gt;0\\\\\n\\lambda\\ge0,\\;\\sum_t\\lambda_t=1}}\n\\frac{\\|XBW(\\lambda)\\|_*^2}{\\mathcal E(X)}.\n\\]</p><p>For an \\(h\\times m\\) matrix \\(M\\), \\(h\\ge m\\), singular-value decomposition gives\n\\[\n\\|M\\|_*=\\max_{UU^{\\mathsf T}=I_m}\\operatorname{tr}(UM).\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(M=P\\Sigma V^{\\mathsf T}\\), complete \\(P\\) to \\(m\\) orthonormal columns. The upper bound follows from diagonal entries of \\(V^{\\mathsf T}UP\\) being at most one, and \\(U=VP^{\\mathsf T}\\) attains it.</p><p>Write \\(a_e=\\langle u_e,Xb_e\\rangle\\). Row signs of \\(U\\) may be changed, so both suprema can be restricted to \\(a_e\\ge0\\). Thus\n\\[\np^*=\\sup_{X,U,\\lambda}\n\\frac{(\\sum_ew_ea_e)^2}{\\mathcal E(X)}.\n\\]\nAn edge lies in at most two parent boundaries \\(O(t)\\): the relevant nodes are the two children of the least hierarchy node containing both endpoints. Put\n\\[\nv_e=\\sum_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathcal M\\\\e\\in O(t)}}\n\\sqrt{\\lambda_t/|O(t)|}.\n\\]\nThen \\(w_e\\le v_e\\le\\sqrt2w_e\\), and\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_ew_ea_e\\right)^2\n\\le\n\\left(\\sum_ev_ea_e\\right)^2\n\\le\n2\\left(\\sum_ew_ea_e\\right)^2.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(X,U\\), setting\n\\[\nc_t=\\frac1{\\sqrt{|O(t)|}}\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}a_e\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\max_{\\lambda\\ge0,\\;\\sum_t\\lambda_t=1}\n\\left(\\sum_ev_ea_e\\right)^2\n=\n\\max_\\lambda\\left(\\sum_t\\sqrt{\\lambda_t}c_t\\right)^2\n=\n\\sum_tc_t^2.\n\\]\nTaking suprema proves \\(p^*\\le Q^*\\le2p^*\\). The final assertion follows from the definition of the finite infimum \\(p^*\\).</p><p>Reversing an edge orientation can be accompanied by reversing the corresponding \\(u_e\\). A contraction \\(UU^{\\mathsf T}\\preceq I_m\\) can be enlarged to\n\\[\n[\\,U\\quad(I_m-UU^{\\mathsf T})^{1/2}\\,]\n\\]\nwhile appending zero rows to \\(X\\), preserving all inner products and the denominator.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite-dimensional separation used in paper Lemma B.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Finite-dimensional separation used in paper Lemma B.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal U\\) is a nonempty open convex subset of a Euclidean space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a\\notin\\mathcal U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a nonzero vector \\(v\\) such that\n\\[\n\\langle v,x-a\\rangle\\ge0\\qquad(x\\in\\mathcal U).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(y\\in\\mathcal U\\), put \\(a_j=a+(a-y)/j\\), and let \\(K=\\overline{\\mathcal U}\\). No \\(a_j\\) belongs to \\(K\\). Otherwise \\(a\\), being a strict convex combination of \\(y\\in\\mathcal U\\) and \\(a_j\\in K\\), would belong to \\(\\mathcal U\\): an open ball about \\(y\\), convexly combined with points of \\(\\mathcal U\\) approaching \\(a_j\\), gives an open ball about \\(a\\) in \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p>Choose a nearest point \\(z_j\\in K\\) to \\(a_j\\). Convexity and differentiation of squared distance give\n\\[\n\\langle z_j-a_j,x-z_j\\rangle\\ge0\\qquad(x\\in K).\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nv_j=\\frac{z_j-a_j}{\\|z_j-a_j\\|_2},\n\\]\nwe obtain\n\\[\n\\langle v_j,x-a_j\\rangle\\ge\\|z_j-a_j\\|_2\\ge0.\n\\]\nA convergent subsequence of these unit vectors has a unit limit \\(v\\), which has the required property.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Matrix infimum used in paper Lemma B.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Matrix infimum used in paper Lemma B.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A,Z\\) are real symmetric positive semidefinite matrices of the same dimension.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\inf_{D\\succ0}\n\\left\\{\\operatorname{tr}(AD^{-1})+\\operatorname{tr}(ZD)\\right\\}\n=\n2\\operatorname{tr}\\left((Z^{1/2}AZ^{1/2})^{1/2}\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First suppose \\(A,Z\\succ0\\). Set\n\\[\nH=Z^{1/2}DZ^{1/2},\\qquad C=Z^{1/2}AZ^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe objective minus \\(2\\operatorname{tr}(C^{1/2})\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(CH^{-1})+\\operatorname{tr}(H)\n-2\\operatorname{tr}(C^{1/2})\n=\n\\|H^{-1/2}C^{1/2}-H^{1/2}\\|_F^2\\ge0.\n\\]\nEquality holds at \\(H=C^{1/2}\\).</p><p>For general \\(A,Z\\succeq0\\), use \\(A_\\varepsilon=A+\\varepsilon I\\) and \\(Z_\\varepsilon=Z+\\varepsilon I\\). Applying the positive definite identity at any fixed \\(D\\), then letting \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\), gives the lower bound. The unperturbed objective is at most the perturbed objective at every \\(D\\), so its infimum is at most the perturbed infimum. Letting \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\) gives the upper bound.</p><p>Continuity of positive semidefinite square roots follows from the spectral theorem: square roots along a convergent sequence are bounded, and every convergent subsequence has the unique positive semidefinite square root of the limit as its limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Spectral packing — paper Lemma B.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Spectral packing — paper Lemma B.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(F\\) is a nonempty set of oriented edges in a finite multigraph with edge set \\(E\\), and \\(m=|F|\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y_v\\in\\mathbb R^E\\), \\(Y_e=Y_u-Y_v\\) for \\(e=(u,v)\\), and \\(Y_{e,e}\\) is the \\(e\\)-coordinate of \\(Y_e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a&gt;0\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\Upsilon=\n  \\left(\\frac1m\\sum_{e\\in F}Y_{e,e}\\right)^2&gt;0,\n  \\qquad\n  \\Upsilon\\ge\\frac am\\sum_{e\\in F}\\|Y_e\\|_2^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A Euclidean ball of squared radius \\(r\\) is\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B_2(y,r)=\\{z:\\|z-y\\|_2^2\\le r\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has \\(a\\le1\\), and there are \\(b\\ge2\\) pairwise disjoint Euclidean balls of one positive squared radius \\(r\\), centered at images of endpoints of edges in \\(F\\), with\n\\[\nb\\ge\\frac{am}{16},\n\\qquad\nbr\\ge\n\\frac{9m\\Upsilon}{512(5+\\log_2(1/a))^2}.\n\\]\nIn particular, for \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/3\\), setting\n\\[\nC_1(\\varepsilon)=1024\\varepsilon^{-2}\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nb\\ge\\frac{a|F|}{C_1(\\varepsilon)},\n\\qquad\nbr\\ge\\frac{a^\\varepsilon\\Upsilon|F|}{C_1(\\varepsilon)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\Upsilon\\le\\frac1m\\sum_{e\\in F}Y_{e,e}^2\n\\le\\frac1m\\sum_{e\\in F}\\|Y_e\\|_2^2,\n\\]\nso \\(a\\le1\\).</p><p>Let \\(P_F:\\mathbb R^E\\to\\mathbb R^F\\) be coordinate restriction, and form the \\(m\\times m\\) matrix \\(A\\) with columns \\(P_FY_e\\). Put \\(\\tau=m\\sqrt\\Upsilon\\). Its singular values \\(\\sigma_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\sigma_m\\ge0\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\sigma_i\\ge|\\operatorname{tr}A|=\\tau,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\sigma_i^2=\\|A\\|_F^2\\le\\frac{\\tau^2}{am}.\n\\]\nThe trace bound follows by writing a singular-value decomposition and using that the inner products of corresponding unit singular vectors have absolute value at most one.</p><p>For any matrix \\(C\\) of rank at most \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\|A-C\\|_F^2\\ge\\sum_{i=k+1}^m\\sigma_i^2.\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(Q\\) projects onto the column space of \\(C\\), then\n\\[\n\\|A-C\\|_F^2\\ge\\|(I-Q)A\\|_F^2\n=\\sum_i\\sigma_i^2(1-\\|Qu_i\\|_2^2).\n\\]\nThe weights \\(\\|Qu_i\\|_2^2\\) lie in \\([0,1]\\), sum to at most \\(k\\), and their weighted sum against decreasing \\(\\sigma_i^2\\) is at most \\(\\sum_{i\\le k}\\sigma_i^2\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\np_0=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{am}{16}\\right\\rfloor+1,\n\\qquad\nT_p=\\sum_{i=p}^m\\sigma_i^2.\n\\]\nThen \\(1\\le p_0\\le m\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&lt;p_0}\\sigma_i\n\\le\\sqrt{(p_0-1)\\sum_i\\sigma_i^2}\\le\\frac\\tau4.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\sum_{i=p_0}^m\\sigma_i\\ge3\\tau/4\\).</p><p>Partition \\([p_0,m]\\cap\\mathbb Z\\) into the nonempty blocks\n\\[\nI_j=\\{2^jp_0,\\ldots,\\min(2^{j+1}p_0-1,m)\\},\n\\]\nwhose number is\n\\[\nL=1+\\left\\lfloor\\log_2(m/p_0)\\right\\rfloor\n\\le5+\\log_2(1/a).\n\\]\nFor a block beginning at \\(p\\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in I_j}\\sigma_i\\le\\sqrt{pT_p}.\n\\]\nSome block therefore begins at an index \\(p\\) satisfying\n\\[\np\\ge p_0&gt;\\frac{am}{16},\n\\qquad\npT_p\\ge\\frac{9\\tau^2}{16L^2}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Choose\n\\[\nr=\\frac{T_p}{32m}.\n\\]\nScan the endpoint images of \\(F\\), retaining a point precisely when its distance from all previously retained points is greater than \\(2\\sqrt r\\). This gives pairwise disjoint closed balls. Every endpoint is within \\(2\\sqrt r\\) of a retained center; denote a chosen center by \\(Y_{c(v)}\\).</p><p>If \\(b\\le p\\), let the \\(e=(u,v)\\) column of \\(C\\) be\n\\[\nP_F(Y_{c(u)}-Y_{c(v)}).\n\\]\nThese columns span a space of dimension at most \\(b-1\\le p-1\\), whereas\n\\[\n\\|A-C\\|_F^2\n\\le\n\\sum_{e=(u,v)\\in F}\n2\\bigl(\\|Y_u-Y_{c(u)}\\|_2^2+\\|Y_v-Y_{c(v)}\\|_2^2\\bigr)\n\\le16mr=\\frac{T_p}{2}.\n\\]\nThis contradicts the rank approximation bound. Thus \\(b\\ge p+1\\ge2\\), and\n\\[\nbr\\ge\\frac{pT_p}{32m}\n\\ge\\frac{9m\\Upsilon}{512L^2}.\n\\]</p><p>For the power form, put \\(t=\\log(1/a)\\ge0\\). Since \\(\\log2\\ge1/2\\),\n\\[\n5+\\log_2(1/a)\\le5+2t.\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\max_{t\\ge0}te^{-\\varepsilon t/2}=2/(e\\varepsilon)\\le1/\\varepsilon\\) and \\(5\\le5/(3\\varepsilon)\\),\n\\[\n(5+\\log_2(1/a))e^{-\\varepsilon t/2}&lt;\\frac4\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nSubstitution yields\n\\[\nbr\\ge\\frac{9\\varepsilon^2}{8192}a^\\varepsilon m\\Upsilon\n\\ge\\frac{\\varepsilon^2}{1024}a^\\varepsilon m\\Upsilon.\n\\]\nAlso \\(C_1(\\varepsilon)\\ge16\\), proving the count bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Geometric bag extraction — paper Proposition B.7.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Geometric bag extraction — paper Proposition B.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) has a hierarchy as in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(X_v\\in\\{0,1\\}^d\\), and \\(U:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^E\\) is a contraction with rows \\(u_e^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(e=(u,v)\\), write\n  \\[\n  X_e=X_u-X_v,\\quad\n  a_e=\\langle u_e,X_e\\rangle\\ge0,\\quad\n  d_e=\\|X_e\\|_2^2=\\|X_e\\|_1.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(a_e^2\\le d_e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A node is \\(\\alpha\\)-bad if\n  \\[\n  \\left(\\frac1{|O(t)|}\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}a_e\\right)^2\n  \\ge\n  \\frac{\\alpha}{|O(t)|}\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}d_e.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(T\\) is a nonempty finite set of \\(\\alpha\\)-bad nodes with positive boundary sums, where \\(0&lt;\\alpha\\le1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An edge set is \\(c\\)-homogeneous if its positive \\(a_e^2\\) values have pairwise ratio less than \\(c\\), and the same holds for its \\(d_e\\) values.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(O'(t)\\subseteq O(t)\\) is \\(\\gamma\\)-dominating if\n  \\[\n  \\left(\\sum_{e\\in O'(t)}a_e\\right)^2\n  \\ge\\gamma\\left(\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}a_e\\right)^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In \\(Q=[0,1]^d\\), put\n  \\[\n  B_Q(x,r)=\\{y\\in Q:\\|y-x\\|_1&lt;r\\}.\n  \\]\n  A bag is a nonempty collection of pairwise disjoint such balls of one radius, centered among the \\(X_v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A bag has type \\((\\delta,\\Delta)\\) if its radius is \\(\\delta\\) and its center diameter is at most \\(\\Delta\\). It is \\(\\beta\\)-compact if\n  \\[\n  |\\mathrm{Bag}|\\ge2,\\qquad \\beta\\Delta\\le\\delta|\\mathrm{Bag}|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A radius-\\(\\delta\\) bag is \\(\\eta\\)-assigned to \\(t\\) if\n  \\[\n  |\\mathrm{Bag}_t|\\ge\\eta|O(t)|,\n  \\]\n  and every ball has a center \\(X_u\\), \\(u\\in V(t)\\), incident to an edge \\(uv\\in O(t)\\) with \\(d_{uv}&lt;\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A family has mutually disjoint balls across all its bags. Assigned bags in one family belong to distinct nodes.</p><p class=\"li\">A sequence of compact families of types \\((\\delta_i,\\Delta_i)\\) is \\(\\lambda\\)-geometric if \\(\\Delta_{i+1}&lt;\\lambda\\delta_i\\). An assigned sequence is \\(\\lambda\\)-geometric if \\(\\delta_{i+1}&lt;\\lambda\\delta_i\\) and its node sets are pairwise disjoint.</p><p class=\"li\">Its mass is\n  \\[\n  \\mathsf M=\\sum_i\\delta_i\\sum_{\\mathrm{Bag}\\in\\mathcal F_i}|\\mathrm{Bag}|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  J=1+\\left\\lceil\\log_2(256/\\alpha^2)\\right\\rceil,\\quad\n  C_2(\\alpha)=128J^4,\\quad s=\\alpha/C_2(\\alpha),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  L=2+\\left\\lceil\\log_2(1/s)\\right\\rceil,\\quad\n  H=1+\\left\\lceil\\log_2(24/(\\lambda s^2))\\right\\rceil,\n  \\quad C_1(\\varepsilon)=1024\\varepsilon^{-2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\beta&gt;1\\), \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/3\\), \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\), and\n  \\[\n  s^\\varepsilon\\le(48\\beta C_1(\\varepsilon))^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a \\(\\lambda\\)-geometric sequence consisting either entirely of \\(\\beta\\)-compact families or entirely of \\(s^{1+2\\varepsilon}\\)-assigned families assigned to distinct nodes of \\(T\\), with\n\\[\n\\mathsf M\\ge\n\\frac{s^\\varepsilon}{384\\beta C_1(\\varepsilon)C_2(\\alpha)LH}\n\\sum_{t\\in T}\\frac1{|O(t)|}\n\\left(\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}a_e\\right)^2.\n\\]\nAll losses depend only on \\(\\alpha,\\varepsilon,\\beta,\\lambda\\), not on the number of vertices or hierarchy depth.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 supplies \\(2\\)-homogeneous subsets with\n\\[\n\\gamma=\\frac1{4J^4}=\\frac{32}{C_2(\\alpha)}.\n\\]\nFor each node put\n\\[\nA_t=\\min_{O'(t)}a_e^2,\\qquad\nB_t=\\min_{O'(t)}d_e,\\qquad\ns_t=\\frac{(\\sum_{O'(t)}a_e)^2}{|O(t)|}.\n\\]\nThe pointwise argument in Lemma 4.2, now using \\(2\\)-homogeneity, gives\n\\[\n1\\le B_t/A_t\\le\\frac2{\\alpha\\gamma}=\\frac1{16s}.\n\\]</p><p>Group nodes by\n\\[\nn=\\lfloor\\log_2A_t\\rfloor,\\qquad m=\\lfloor\\log_2B_t\\rfloor.\n\\]\nFor each \\(n\\), at most \\(L\\) values of \\(m\\) occur. Retain the group maximizing the sum of \\(s_t\\), and call it \\(T_n\\). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_n\\sum_{t\\in T_n}s_t\n\\ge\\frac1{C_2(\\alpha)L}\n\\sum_{t\\in T}\\frac{(\\sum_{O(t)}a_e)^2}{|O(t)|}.\n\\]\nThe union of \\(O'(t)\\) over \\(T_n\\) is \\(4\\)-homogeneous. Lemma 4.2 supplies a family of mass at least\n\\[\n\\frac{s^\\varepsilon}{192\\beta C_1(\\varepsilon)}\n\\sum_{t\\in T_n}s_t,\n\\]\nwhose radius lies between \\(s2^n\\) and \\(24\\,2^n/s\\); the latter also bounds its compact diameter.</p><p>Partition these families by \\(n\\bmod H\\) and by whether they are compact or assigned. Some one of the \\(2H\\) classes has at least \\(1/(2H)\\) of the total mass. Order it by decreasing \\(n\\). Successive indices satisfy \\(n'\\le n-H\\), so\n\\[\n\\frac{24\\,2^{n'}}s\n\\le\\frac{24\\,2^{n-H}}s\n&lt;\\lambda s2^n.\n\\]\nThis proves the geometric condition. Assigned node sets remain disjoint because the \\(T_n\\) are disjoint. Combining the mass bounds proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Two elementary truncations — paper Lemma B.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Two elementary truncations — paper Lemma B.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the embedding, edge quantities, and badness definition of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  J=1+\\left\\lceil\\log_2(256/\\alpha^2)\\right\\rceil,\\qquad\n  \\gamma=\\frac1{4J^4}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(\\alpha\\)-bad node with a positive boundary sum has a \\(2\\)-homogeneous, \\(\\gamma\\)-dominating subset \\(O'(t)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the node, let \\(m=|O(t)|\\), and take expectations uniformly over \\(O(t)\\). With \\(\\mu=\\mathbb E a_e&gt;0\\), badness says \\(\\mathbb E d_e\\le\\mu^2/\\alpha\\).</p><p>Discard edges with \\(a_e&lt;\\mu/4\\) or \\(d_e&gt;16\\mu^2/\\alpha^2\\). The first class contributes at most \\(\\mu/4\\) to \\(\\mathbb E a_e\\). Markov's inequality bounds the second class's probability by \\(\\alpha/16\\), and Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[a_e\\mathbf1_{\\{d_e&gt;16\\mu^2/\\alpha^2\\}}]\n\\le\n\\sqrt{\\mathbb E a_e^2\\,\n\\mathbb P(d_e&gt;16\\mu^2/\\alpha^2)}\n\\le\\mu/4.\n\\]\nThe remaining edges contribute at least \\(m\\mu/2\\), and both \\(a_e^2\\) and \\(d_e\\) lie in\n\\[\n[\\mu^2/16,\\;16\\mu^2/\\alpha^2].\n\\]\nDivide this interval into half-open dyadic intervals, with an extra last interval if needed. There are at most \\(J\\) choices for each quantity and \\(J^2\\) pairs. Some pair has \\(a_e\\)-sum at least \\(m\\mu/(2J^2)\\). Squaring gives the stated dominance.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**One homogeneous family — paper Lemma B.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**One homogeneous family — paper Lemma B.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 4, but assume directly that \\(O'(t)\\) is \\(\\gamma\\)-dominating for every \\(t\\in T\\), \\(0&lt;\\gamma\\le1\\), and\n  \\[\n  F=\\bigcup_{t\\in T}O'(t)\n  \\]\n  is \\(4\\)-homogeneous.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  s=\\alpha\\gamma/32,\\quad\n  c_1=\\min_{e\\in F}a_e^2,\\quad\n  S'=\\sum_{t\\in T}\\frac1{|O(t)|}\n  \\left(\\sum_{e\\in O'(t)}a_e\\right)^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C=C_1(\\varepsilon)\\), where \\(\\beta&gt;1\\), \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/3\\), and\n  \\[\n  s^\\varepsilon\\le(48\\beta C)^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is either a family of \\(\\beta\\)-compact bags or a family of \\(s^{1+2\\varepsilon}\\)-assigned bags assigned to a subset of \\(T\\), with mass at least\n\\[\n\\frac{s^\\varepsilon}{192\\beta C}S'.\n\\]\nIts radius, and its compact diameter parameter when applicable, lie in \\([sc_1,12c_1/s]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nm_t=|O(t)|,\\quad m'_t=|O'(t)|,\\quad\nN=\\sum_tm_t,\\quad N'=|F|,\\quad c_2=\\max_{e\\in F}d_e.\n\\]\nHomogeneity, dominance, and badness give\n\\[\n4c_1(m'_t)^2\n\\ge\n\\left(\\sum_{O'(t)}a_e\\right)^2\n\\ge\n\\gamma\\left(\\sum_{O(t)}a_e\\right)^2\n\\ge\n\\alpha\\gamma m_t\\sum_{O(t)}d_e\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\alpha\\gamma}{4}m_tm'_tc_2.\n\\]\nAfter canceling \\(m'_t\\), summing, and using \\(\\sum_tm'_t\\le2N'\\), obtain\n\\[\nc_1N'\\ge sNc_2.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\ns\\le\\widetilde\\alpha:=sN/N'\\le c_1/c_2\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(Y_v=UX_v\\). Then\n\\[\n(\\mathbb E_{e\\in F}a_e)^2\n\\ge c_1\n\\ge\\widetilde\\alpha c_2\n\\ge\\widetilde\\alpha\\,\\mathbb E_{e\\in F}\\|Y_e\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nProposition 3 gives \\(b\\) disjoint Euclidean balls of squared radius \\(r\\), with\n\\[\nb\\ge\\widetilde\\alpha N'/C,\\qquad\nbr\\ge\\widetilde\\alpha^\\varepsilon c_1N'/C.\n\\]\nContraction and the binary-coordinate identity imply that the corresponding \\(Q\\)-balls of radius \\(r\\) are disjoint. Shrink to radius \\(\\delta\\) so that\n\\[\nb\\delta=w:=\\widetilde\\alpha^\\varepsilon c_1N'/C.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(2N'\\) endpoint centers, hence\n\\[\n\\frac{s^\\varepsilon c_1}{2C}\\le\\delta\n\\le c_1\\widetilde\\alpha^{\\varepsilon-1}\\le c_1/s.\n\\]\nThe smallness hypothesis implies \\(s^{1-\\varepsilon}\\le s^\\varepsilon\\le1/(2C)\\), so\n\\[\nsc_1\\le\\delta\\le c_1/s.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\nS'\\le4c_1\\sum_t\\frac{(m'_t)^2}{m_t}\n\\le8c_1N'\n\\le8Cs^{-\\varepsilon}w.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(V'(t)\\) be the endpoints of \\(O'(t)\\) inside \\(V(t)\\), and \\(V'=\\bigcup_tV'(t)\\). Set\n\\[\nD=\\max\\{\\delta,2c_2\\}.\n\\]\nChoose a maximal disjoint collection \\(\\mathcal B\\) of radius-\\(D\\) balls centered at \\(X_u\\), \\(u\\in V'\\). Every such center is within \\(2D\\) of a chosen center. Every small-ball center is within \\(c_2\\) of \\(V'\\), hence within \\(3D\\) of a chosen center.</p><p>If \\(|\\mathcal B|&lt;w/(12\\beta D)\\), assign each small ball to its nearest chosen center. Each resulting bag has center diameter at most \\(6D\\). Retain bags containing at least \\(6\\beta D/\\delta\\) balls. Fewer than \\(b/2\\) balls are discarded. Retained bags are \\(\\beta\\)-compact of type \\((\\delta,6D)\\), and have mass at least \\(w/2\\).</p><p>Otherwise assign each ball of \\(\\mathcal B\\) to a node \\(t\\) whose \\(V'(t)\\) contains its center. Keep bags satisfying\n\\[\n|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|\\ge\\frac{|\\mathcal B|m_t}{2N}.\n\\]\nFewer than half the balls are lost. Their centers have an edge in \\(O'(t)\\) of length at most \\(c_2&lt;D\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|}{m_t}\\ge\\frac{w}{24\\beta DN}.\n\\]\nIf \\(D=\\delta\\), this is at least \\(s/(24\\beta C)\\); if \\(D=2c_2\\), it is at least \\(s^{1+\\varepsilon}/(48\\beta C)\\). The smallness hypothesis makes both at least \\(s^{1+2\\varepsilon}\\). The retained mass is at least \\(w/(24\\beta)\\).</p><p>In either case the bound on \\(S'\\) proves the required mass. Since \\(c_2\\le c_1/s\\), one has \\(D\\le2c_1/s\\), giving all stated scale bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Compact-bag charging — paper Proposition B.8.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Compact-bag charging — paper Proposition B.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G=(V,E)\\) is a finite \\(k\\)-edge-connected multigraph, \\(k&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(X_v\\in Q=[0,1]^h\\), and \\(X_{\\{u,v\\}}=X_u-X_v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\((\\mathcal F_i)\\) is a finite or countable sequence of nonempty families of \\(\\beta\\)-compact bags, with types \\((\\delta_i,\\Delta_i)\\) as defined in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">All balls in each family are mutually disjoint, and\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\lambda\\le1/12,\\qquad \\beta\\ge36,\\qquad\n  \\beta\\Delta_i\\le|\\mathrm D|\\delta_i,\\qquad\n  \\Delta_{i+1}\\le\\lambda\\delta_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An ordinary ball is \\(B_Q(x,b)\\), including its center. A hollowed ball is the strict shell\n  \\[\n  B_Q(x;a,b)=\\{y\\in Q:a&lt;\\|y-x\\|_1&lt;b\\},\n  \\quad0\\le a&lt;b.\n  \\]\n  Its width is \\(b-a\\); an ordinary radius-\\(b\\) ball has numerical inner radius \\(0\\) and width \\(b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac k4\\sum_i\\delta_i\\sum_{\\mathrm D\\in\\mathcal F_i}|\\mathrm D|\n\\le\\sum_{e\\in E}\\|X_e\\|_1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>It suffices to consider a finite initial sequence \\(\\mathcal F_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal F_q\\), and set \\(\\Delta_{q+1}=0\\). Disjoint radius-\\(\\delta_i\\) balls have centers at distance at least \\(2\\delta_i\\), by their midpoint in \\(Q\\). Hence\n\\[\n2\\delta_i\\le\\Delta_i,\\qquad\n6\\Delta_{i+1}\\le\\delta_i/2,\\qquad\n|\\mathrm D|\\delta_i\\ge\\beta\\Delta_i.\n\\]</p><p>Construct a finite live collection \\(\\mathcal Z\\), initially empty, processing scales in order. During phase \\(i\\), original balls inserted from \\(\\mathcal F_i\\) are fresh; other objects, including shells split from old objects, are old. A fresh ball remains intact throughout its phase. Put\n\\[\np_i(H)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\delta_i-6\\Delta_{i+1},&amp;H\\text{ fresh},\\\\{}\n[w(H)-6\\Delta_i]_+,&amp;H\\text{ old},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nP_i(\\mathcal Z)=\\sum_Hp_i(H).\n\\]\nMaintain:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. live objects are pairwise disjoint;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. each has an embedded-vertex center and the two witnesses of Lemma 5.1;\n3.\n   \\[\n   P_i(\\mathcal Z)\\ge\\frac14\n   \\sum_{\\mathrm D\\text{ processed}}|\\mathrm D|\\delta(\\mathrm D).\n   \\]</p><p>For an unprocessed ball \\(B_Q(z,\\delta_i)\\), call it interior to an object with center \\(x\\), radii \\(a,b\\), if\n\\[\na+\\delta_i+\\Delta_i\\le\\|z-x\\|_1\n\\le b-\\delta_i-\\Delta_i.\n\\]\nIts containing object is unique and old.</p><p>If a bag \\(\\mathrm D=\\{B_Q(z_j,\\delta_i):1\\le j\\le n\\}\\) has an interior ball in \\(H\\), put\n\\[\nL=\\min_j\\|z_j-x\\|_1,\\qquad R=\\max_j\\|z_j-x\\|_1.\n\\]\nThe diameter bound gives\n\\[\na+\\delta_i\\le L\\le R\\le b-\\delta_i,\\qquad R-L\\le\\Delta_i.\n\\]\nReplace \\(H\\) by\n\\[\nB_Q(x;a,L-\\delta_i),\\qquad\nB_Q(x;R+\\delta_i,b),\n\\]\ndiscard zero-width shells, insert all balls of \\(\\mathrm D\\), and mark the bag processed.</p><p>All replacements lie in \\(H\\). Strict ball inequalities separate them. An old inner witness and a center achieving \\(L\\) witness the inner shell; a center achieving \\(R\\) and an old outer witness witness the outer shell. An inserted ball uses its center and another center of the bag.</p><p>Writing \\(w=b-a\\) and \\(t=[w-6\\Delta_i]_+\\), inserted balls contribute at least \\(n\\delta_i/2\\). If \\(t=0\\), this suffices. If \\(t&gt;0\\), the replacement capacity is at least\n\\[\nw-(R-L)-2\\delta_i-12\\Delta_i+n\\delta_i/2\n\\ge t+n\\delta_i/2-8\\Delta_i\n\\ge t+n\\delta_i/4,\n\\]\nusing \\(\\beta\\ge36\\). Thus every invariant survives. Repeat until no unprocessed bag has an interior ball.</p><p>Set \\(s_i=2\\delta_i+\\Delta_i\\). Retain fresh balls; shrink each old object to\n\\[\nB_Q(x;a+s_i,b-s_i)\n\\]\nif its width exceeds \\(2s_i\\), otherwise discard it. Insert all balls in the remaining bags. Regard every object as old for phase \\(i+1\\).</p><p>Failure of the interior test says that each remaining ball is wholly below the new inner radius or wholly above the new outer radius, so separation holds. Shrinking preserves witnesses, and inserted balls again use another center in their bag.</p><p>A previously fresh ball has capacity \\(\\delta_i-6\\Delta_{i+1}\\) before and after. For an old object,\n\\[\n2s_i+6\\Delta_{i+1}\n\\le2\\Delta_i+\\frac92\\delta_i\n\\le\\frac{17}4\\Delta_i\n\\le6\\Delta_i.\n\\]\nThus its new capacity is at least its old capacity. A discarded object has old capacity zero, since its width is at most \\(2s_i\\le4\\Delta_i\\). New balls contribute at least \\(\\delta_i/2\\) each, paying the required quarter of their bag mass.</p><p>After the final phase, capacity equals width. Lemma 5.1 yields\n\\[\n\\frac14\\sum_{i=1}^q\\delta_i\\sum_{\\mathrm D\\in\\mathcal F_i}|\\mathrm D|\n\\le\\sum_{H\\in\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{fin}}}w(H)\n\\le\\frac1k\\sum_e\\|X_e\\|_1.\n\\]\nFor a countable sequence, take the supremum over finite initial sums.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Length bound for disjoint hollowed balls — paper Lemma B.9.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Length bound for disjoint hollowed balls — paper Lemma B.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) and \\(X\\) satisfy Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal Z\\) is a finite pairwise disjoint collection of ordinary or hollowed balls.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(H\\in\\mathcal Z\\), its center and numerical radii are \\(x_H,a_H,b_H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">There are vertices \\(u_H,v_H\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\|X_{u_H}-x_H\\|_1\\le a_H,\\qquad\n  \\|X_{v_H}-x_H\\|_1\\ge b_H.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nk\\sum_{H\\in\\mathcal Z}(b_H-a_H)\n\\le\\sum_{\\{u,v\\}\\in E}\\|X_u-X_v\\|_1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Clamp radial distance by\n\\[\nf_H(y)=\\min\\{b_H,\\max\\{a_H,\\|y-x_H\\|_1\\}\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(a_H&lt;t&lt;b_H\\), the set\n\\[\nS_{H,t}=\\{v:\\|X_v-x_H\\|_1&lt;t\\}\n\\]\nis nonempty and proper. Integrating its cut bound gives\n\\[\nk(b_H-a_H)\n\\le\n\\int_{a_H}^{b_H}|\\delta_G(S_{H,t})|\\,dt\n=\n\\sum_{\\{u,v\\}\\in E}|f_H(X_u)-f_H(X_v)|.\n\\]</p><p>Fix an edge, let \\(L_e=\\|X_u-X_v\\|_1\\), and parameterize its segment by\n\\[\n\\gamma(s)=(1-s)X_u+sX_v,\\quad0\\le s\\le1.\n\\]\nEach \\(g_H=f_H\\circ\\gamma\\) is continuous, finitely piecewise affine, and \\(L_e\\)-Lipschitz. Take a common finite subdivision into affine intervals. If \\(g_H\\) is nonconstant on one such interval, the corresponding interior points of \\(\\gamma\\) lie in \\(H\\). Disjointness permits at most one nonconstant \\(g_H\\) on that interval. Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_H|g_H(1)-g_H(0)|\n\\le\n\\sum_jL_e(s_j-s_{j-1})=L_e.\n\\]\nThis also handles a segment along a shell boundary, where the clamped radial function is constant. Sum over edges.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Assigned-bag charging — paper Proposition B.10.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Assigned-bag charging — paper Proposition B.10.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X:V\\to\\{0,1\\}^d\\), \\(Q=[0,1]^d\\), and \\(\\mathcal T\\) is a locally \\(k\\)-connected hierarchy: every induced graph \\(G[V(t)]\\) is \\(k\\)-edge-connected, with singleton conditions vacuous.</p><p class=\"li\">Selected nonroot nodes \\(T\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  |O(t)|\\ge k\\lambda|P(t)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal F_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal F_m\\) is a \\(\\lambda\\)-geometric sequence of \\((24C_3/k)\\)-assigned families, with radii \\(\\delta_i\\) and pairwise disjoint node sets \\(T_i\\subseteq T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  C_4\\ge3,\\qquad\n  \\lambda\\le\\frac1{6C_4},\\qquad\n  C_3\\ge2\\bigl((C_4+1)+4(C_4+2)^2\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Geometric intersections are in the continuous cube. Ordinary balls include their centers; hollow balls have strict inner and outer boundaries. Every center is inherited from an input ball, hence is binary, so \\(y\\mapsto\\|x-y\\|_1\\) is affine on \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A ball record has an owner \\(t(B)\\). A nonavoiding record routes in \\(G[V(t(B))]\\). An avoiding record also has a strict descendant \\(t_d(B)\\), and routes in\n  \\[\n  G[V(t(B))\\setminus V(t_d(B))].\n  \\]\n  Both have a set \\(t_P(B)\\) of strict descendants of the owner.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal T[t]\\) denote the subtree rooted at \\(t\\). Define\n  \\[\n  C(B)=\\mathcal T[t(B)]\\setminus\\bigcup_{s\\in t_P(B)}\\mathcal T[s]\n  \\]\n  for nonavoiding records, and remove \\(\\mathcal T[t_d(B)]\\) as well for avoiding records. Put \\(L(B)=C(B)\\cap V\\), identifying leaves with vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">A collection is valid, as in paper Definition B.12, when:</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(\\mathrm{L1}\\): labels have the stated form, and \\(t(B)\\in C(B)\\);</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(\\mathrm{L2}\\): for every \\(s\\in t_P(B)\\), both endpoints of every edge of \\(P(s)\\) have distance at least the outer radius from the center;</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(\\mathrm{L3}\\): some \\(u\\in L(B)\\) is at distance at most the inner radius and has at least \\(k/4\\) pairwise edge-disjoint paths, in the stated routing graph, to vertices at distance at least the outer radius;</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(\\mathrm{L4}\\): geometrically intersecting distinct records have disjoint conflict domains.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t\\in T_i\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Pred}(t)=\n  \\{s:\\ s\\text{ is a strict descendant of }t,\\ s\\in T_j\n  \\text{ for some }j&lt;i\\}.\n  \\]\n  An input ball \\(B(X_u,\\delta_i)\\in\\mathrm{Bag}_t\\) is noninsertable by \\(s\\) if \\(s\\in\\operatorname{Pred}(t)\\) and an endpoint of an edge of \\(P(s)\\) lies in the ball. It is insertable if no predecessor does this.</p><p class=\"li\">An insertable input ball has nonavoiding labels\n  \\[\n  t(B)=t,\\qquad t_P(B)=\\operatorname{Pred}(t).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In the arrangement subargument below, the assignment assumption is changed to \\(12C_3/k\\), and all input balls are assumed insertable. Families are processed in increasing scale index; bags within a phase are processed in increasing hierarchy depth.</p><p class=\"li\">In phase \\(\\ell\\), a candidate \\(A=B(X_u,\\delta_\\ell)\\in\\mathrm{Bag}_t\\) is interior to a current record with center \\(x\\) and radii \\(r_1&lt;r_2\\) if\n  \\[\n  C(A)\\cap C(B)\\ne\\varnothing,\\qquad\n  r_1+C_3\\delta_\\ell&lt;\\|X_u-x\\|_1&lt;r_2-C_3\\delta_\\ell.\n  \\]\n  Other candidates are border balls.</p><p class=\"li\">The arrangement operations retain original current-phase balls unchanged. Other replacements are concentric radial restrictions with conflict domains contained in the replaced domain. New owners are either the old owner or the current node \\(t\\); a new record with owner \\(t\\) is nonavoiding, and new avoiding records retain the old owner and avoid \\(t\\). The lemmas below verify these requirements for the construction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_{\\{u,v\\}\\in E}\\|X_u-X_v\\|_1\n\\ge\n\\frac k8\\frac{C_4}{12C_3}\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\delta_i\\sum_{t\\in T_i}|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|.\n\\]\nOne may take \\(C_4=3\\), \\(C_3=10^4\\), and \\(\\lambda\\le1/18\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nM=\\sum_i\\delta_i\\sum_{t\\in T_i}|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|.\n\\]\nSumming Lemma 6.2 over predecessors bounds the total radius \\(N\\) of noninsertable balls by\n\\[\nN\\le\\frac4{C_3}M\\le M/4.\n\\]\nA ball may be counted several times, which only enlarges this upper bound.</p><p>Delete all noninsertable balls. Retain a bag if at least half its balls remain; otherwise delete its bag and selected node. A retained bag is \\(12C_3/k\\)-assigned. The total removed mass is at most \\(2N\\), so the remaining mass \\(M'\\) satisfies\n\\[\nM'\\ge M-2N\\ge M/2.\n\\]\nDiscard empty families. Disjointness and the geometric condition remain. Deleting selected nodes only removes predecessors, so retained balls remain insertable.</p><p>Lemma 6.11 supplies a valid collection of total width at least \\(C_4M'/(12C_3)\\). Lemma 6.3 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_e\\|X_e\\|_1\n\\ge\\frac k4\\frac{C_4}{12C_3}M'\n\\ge\\frac k8\\frac{C_4}{12C_3}M.\n\\]\nFor a countable sequence, apply the finite statement to initial segments and take the increasing limit of their masses.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Edge-disjoint paths from cut bounds — paper Lemma B.11.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Edge-disjoint paths from cut bounds — paper Lemma B.11.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(u,v\\) are distinct vertices of an undirected multigraph.</p><p class=\"li\">Every cut separating them has at least \\(q\\) edges, where \\(q\\) is a positive integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(q\\) pairwise edge-disjoint \\(u\\)-\\(v\\) paths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Orient edges arbitrarily and maintain an integral antisymmetric flow whose signed value on an edge belongs to \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}\\), initially zero. A directed traversal is available when its signed flow in that direction is less than one.</p><p>While the value is below \\(q\\), augment along an available directed \\(u\\)-\\(v\\) path. If no such path exists, let \\(S\\) be the vertices reachable from \\(u\\) by available traversals. Then \\(v\\notin S\\), and each edge leaving \\(S\\) has outward flow one. Summing conservation over \\(S\\) makes the current value equal to the cut size, at least \\(q\\), a contradiction.</p><p>After \\(q\\) augmentations, extract a directed positive-flow \\(u\\)-\\(v\\) path and subtract one on it. Such a path exists by the same reachable-set conservation argument. Repeat \\(q\\) times. Each extracted edge then has flow zero, so the paths are edge-disjoint.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cost of one predecessor — paper Lemma B.13.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Cost of one predecessor — paper Lemma B.13.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the hierarchy, scales, predecessors, and noninsertability definitions of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">For this lemma assume only \\(\\lambda\\le1/2\\) and assignment strength at least \\(C_3/k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B_i\\) denote all input balls at scale \\(\\delta_i\\), and fix \\(s\\in T_\\ell\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_i\\delta_i\n|\\{B\\in\\mathcal B_i:B\\text{ is noninsertable by }s\\}|\n\\le\\frac4{C_3}\\delta_\\ell|\\mathrm{Bag}_s|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The counted set is empty for \\(i\\le\\ell\\). At each later scale, assign each counted ball an endpoint of an edge of \\(P(s)\\) lying in it. Disjoint center-inclusive balls make this assignment injective on endpoint vertices, so there are at most \\(2|P(s)|\\) balls. Thus\n\\[\n2|P(s)|\\sum_{i&gt;\\ell}\\delta_i\n\\le\\frac{2\\lambda}{1-\\lambda}|P(s)|\\delta_\\ell\n\\le4\\lambda|P(s)|\\delta_\\ell\n\\le\\frac4k|O(s)|\\delta_\\ell\n\\le\\frac4{C_3}|\\mathrm{Bag}_s|\\delta_\\ell.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Charging a valid labeled collection — paper Lemma B.14.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Charging a valid labeled collection — paper Lemma B.14.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph embedding and valid-labeling definition of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Z\\) is a finite valid collection of ordinary and hollow ball records.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac k4\\sum_{B\\in Z}\\operatorname{width}(B)\n\\le\\sum_{\\{v,w\\}\\in E}\\|X_v-X_w\\|_1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Realize each graph edge by its fixed straight segment in \\(Q\\), of length \\(\\ell_e=\\|X_v-X_w\\|_1\\). Parallel edges remain distinct resources.</p><p>For a record \\(B\\) with center \\(x\\), radii \\(a&lt;b\\), stop each path from \\(\\mathrm{L3}\\) at its first vertex of distance at least \\(b\\). These paths stay in \\(L(B)\\). Indeed, their routing graph already excludes \\(t_d(B)\\). A first entrance into \\(V(s)\\), \\(s\\in t_P(B)\\), would use an edge of \\(P(s)\\) whose preceding endpoint has distance less than \\(b\\), contradicting \\(\\mathrm{L2}\\).</p><p>On each resulting continuous curve, retain the portion from the last radius-\\(a\\) point preceding its first radius-\\(b\\) point to that first radius-\\(b\\) point. It lies in the open shell except at endpoints and has length at least \\(b-a\\), since radial distance is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz. The same argument applies to an ordinary ball with \\(a=0\\). One record charges no edge portion twice, since its paths are edge-disjoint.</p><p>If two records charge portions of one graph edge, both endpoints belong to both leaf domains, so the conflict domains meet. Condition \\(\\mathrm{L4}\\) forces their geometric balls to be disjoint; thus their charged open edge portions are disjoint. Each edge is charged at most \\(\\ell_e\\), while each record receives at least \\((k/4)(b-a)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Validity of an insertable input ball — paper Lemma B.16.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Validity of an insertable input ball — paper Lemma B.16.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arrangement setting of Proposition 6: all input balls are insertable and each bag is \\(12C_3/k\\)-assigned.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B=B(X_u,\\delta_i)\\in\\mathrm{Bag}_t\\) has its input labels.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(B\\) satisfies \\(\\mathrm{L1}\\)–\\(\\mathrm{L3}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The labels give \\(\\mathrm{L1}\\), and insertability gives \\(\\mathrm{L2}\\).</p><p>The center vertex \\(u\\) lies in \\(L(B)\\). To see this, choose its assigned edge \\(uv\\in O(t)\\). If \\(u\\in V(s)\\) for a predecessor \\(s\\), that edge belongs to \\(P(s)\\), and its endpoint \\(u\\) lies in \\(B\\), contradicting insertability.</p><p>Local connectivity gives \\(|O(t)|\\ge k\\), so\n\\[\n|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|\\ge12C_3|O(t)|/k\\ge12C_3&gt;1.\n\\]\nChoose another ball center \\(X_w\\), \\(w\\in V(t)\\). Disjointness puts \\(X_w\\) outside \\(B\\). Lemma 6.1 in the \\(k\\)-edge-connected graph \\(G[V(t)]\\) supplies \\(k\\) edge-disjoint \\(u\\)-\\(w\\) paths. These prove \\(\\mathrm{L3}\\), with \\(k\\) in place of \\(k/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Ancestor relation — paper Lemma B.17.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Ancestor relation — paper Lemma B.17.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arrangement setting, processing order, labels, and permitted operations of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm{Bag}_t\\) is being processed in phase \\(\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A candidate \\(A\\in\\mathrm{Bag}_t\\) and current record \\(B\\) have intersecting conflict domains.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(t(B)\\) is a weak ancestor of \\(t\\), and \\(t\\in C(B)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Intersecting rooted connected subtrees have comparable roots. A current owner in \\(T_\\ell\\) cannot be a proper descendant of \\(t\\), by depth order. If an owner \\(s\\in T_i\\), \\(i&lt;\\ell\\), were a proper descendant of \\(t\\), then \\(s\\in\\operatorname{Pred}(t)=t_P(A)\\); its entire subtree, hence \\(C(B)\\), would be disjoint from \\(C(A)\\).</p><p>Thus \\(t(B)\\) lies above \\(t\\). Connectedness of \\(C(B)\\) shows that the path from its root to a common node passes through \\(t\\), so \\(t\\in C(B)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Unique interior record — paper Lemma B.18.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Unique interior record — paper Lemma B.18.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arrangement setting of Proposition 6 and a valid current collection.</p><p class=\"li\">A candidate \\(A\\in\\mathrm{Bag}_t\\) satisfies \\(C(A)\\cap C(B)\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Another current record \\(D\\ne B\\) geometrically intersects \\(B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(C(A)\\cap C(D)=\\varnothing\\). Consequently a candidate is interior to at most one current record.</p>", "proof": "<p>If both conflict intersections were nonempty, Lemma 6.5 would give\n\\[\nt\\in C(B)\\cap C(D),\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(\\mathrm{L4}\\). A candidate interior to both records is contained in both, so their supports intersect.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Inheritance of separation — paper Lemma B.19.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Inheritance of separation — paper Lemma B.19.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(B,D\\) satisfy \\(\\mathrm{L4}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A new record \\(B'\\) has support contained in \\(B\\) and \\(C(B')\\subseteq C(B)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(B',D\\) satisfy \\(\\mathrm{L4}\\). A positive-width concentric radial restriction of a record, retaining its labels, also retains \\(\\mathrm{L1}\\)–\\(\\mathrm{L3}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>An intersection of \\(B'\\) with \\(D\\) is an intersection of \\(B\\) with \\(D\\), and the new conflict intersection is a subset of the old one.</p><p>For a concentric restriction, excluded boundary endpoints remain outside the smaller outer radius. The old inner witness lies inside the new inner radius, and the old paths cross the restricted radial interval. Labels and routing domains are unchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Border balls stay border balls — paper Lemma B.20.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Border balls stay border balls — paper Lemma B.20.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arrangement setting and permitted operations of Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>During a phase, a border candidate never becomes interior. In particular, a border ball retained after processing its bag remains border at the phase's end.</p>", "proof": "<p>A newly inserted original ball has radius \\(\\delta_\\ell\\) and cannot contain a candidate with the two \\(C_3\\delta_\\ell\\) radial margins, since \\(C_3&gt;1\\).</p><p>Every other new record is a concentric radial restriction of an old record with a subset of its conflict domain. A candidate satisfying the interior test for the new record therefore satisfied it for the old record. Iterating proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.9", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Potential preserved during interior processing — paper Lemma B.21.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Potential preserved during interior processing — paper Lemma B.21.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the all-insertable, \\(12C_3/k\\)-assigned arrangement setting of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(\\delta_{m+1}=0\\), \\([z]_+=\\max\\{z,0\\}\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\theta=\\frac{C_4}{6C_3}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In phase \\(\\ell\\), a record of width \\(b-a\\) has potential\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(A)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  \\delta_\\ell-C_4\\delta_{\\ell+1},\n  &amp;A\\text{ is an original current-family ball},\\\\{}\n  [b-a-C_4\\delta_\\ell]_+,\n  &amp;A\\text{ is other nonavoiding},\\\\{}\n  [b-a-C_4\\delta_\\ell]_+/(2(C_4+2)),\n  &amp;A\\text{ is avoiding}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For the bag currently processed, \\(\\mathrm{Int}_t\\) and \\(\\mathrm{Bor}_t\\) are untreated interior and border balls. After \\(s\\) operations,\n  \\[\n  n_{t,s}=|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|-|\\mathrm{Bor}_{t,s}|-|\\mathrm{Int}_{t,s}|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Phase \\(\\ell\\) starts with a valid arrangement whose potential can pay each earlier node \\(v\\in T_i\\), \\(i&lt;\\ell\\), at least\n  \\[\n  (\\theta/2)|\\mathrm{Bag}_v|\\delta_i.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Interior processing preserves validity and all earlier allocations. It pays each completed current-phase node at least \\(\\theta n_{v,\\infty}\\delta_\\ell\\), and the current node at least \\(\\theta n_{t,s}\\delta_\\ell\\). Each bag finishes with\n\\[\n|\\mathrm{Int}_{t,\\infty}|&lt;|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|/2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The constant assumptions imply\n\\[\nC_3-1\\ge2\\{C_4+4(C_4+2)^2\\},\n\\quad\n\\frac78(C_3-1)\\ge C_4+4,\n\\]\n\\[\nC_3\\ge2C_4(C_4+2),\n\\qquad\n\\theta\\le\\frac1{12(C_4+2)}\\le\\frac1{60}.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n\\delta_\\ell-C_4\\delta_{\\ell+1}\\ge\\frac56\\delta_\\ell.\n\\]\nEvery potential is at most width.</p><p>Maintain actual allocations. When removing an old record \\(B\\), reserve at least \\(\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B)\\) from replacements for its former allocations. Additional funds go to the current node. Lemma 6.7 handles radial restrictions. Original current-family balls are never carved: their width is \\(\\delta_\\ell\\), while an interior-containing record has width greater than \\(2C_3\\delta_\\ell\\).</p><p>Fix a bag, write \\(\\delta=\\delta_\\ell\\), and continue while\n\\[\n|\\mathrm{Int}_t|\\ge|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|/2.\n\\]\nAfter each operation remove the treated balls, and transfer candidates no longer interior to the border set. Lemma 6.8 makes this transfer permanent.</p><p>**Operation 1: an avoiding shell.** Suppose an interior ball \\(A=B(X_u,\\delta)\\) lies in an avoiding record \\(B=B(x;r_1,r_2)\\). Put \\(q=\\|X_u-x\\|_1\\). Replace \\(B\\) by\n\\[\nB_1=B(x;r_1,q-\\delta),\\qquad\nB_2=B(x;q+\\delta,r_2)\n\\]\nwith its labels, and insert \\(A\\) with its input labels. Residual shells are disjoint from \\(A\\); Lemmas 6.4, 6.6, and 6.7 prove validity.</p><p>For \\(R=r_2-r_1&gt;2C_3\\delta\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B_1)+\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B_2)+\\delta/2\n\\ge\n\\frac{R-2\\delta-2C_4\\delta}{2(C_4+2)}+\\delta/2\n=\n\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B).\n\\]\nReserve those amounts for \\(B\\). The inserted ball retains at least\n\\[\n\\delta-C_4\\delta_{\\ell+1}-\\delta/2\\ge\\delta/3\\ge\\theta\\delta\n\\]\nfor \\(t\\).</p><p>**Common setup for Operations 2 and 3.** Assume no interior ball is in an avoiding shell. Let\n\\[\nH=G[V(t^*)\\setminus V(t)],\n\\quad\nO'(t)=\\{uv\\in O(t):\\|X_u-X_v\\|_1&lt;\\delta\\}.\n\\]\nRepeatedly split \\(V(H)\\) along cuts of size less than \\(k/4\\), obtaining induced \\(k/4\\)-edge-connected sets \\(S_1,\\ldots,S_j\\), with singletons allowed. The \\(j-1\\) splits imply\n\\[\n\\sum_i|\\partial_HS_i|&lt;\\frac k2(j-1)\\quad(j&gt;1),\n\\]\nand the sum is zero for \\(j=1\\). Local connectivity of \\(G[V(t^*)]\\) gives\n\\[\njk\\le |O(t)|+\\sum_i|\\partial_HS_i|\n\\le |O(t)|+\\frac k2(j-1),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nj\\le2|O(t)|/k.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(U\\) be the current interior center vertices, and set\n\\[\nU_i=\\{u\\in U:uv\\in O'(t)\\text{ for some }v\\in S_i\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nV_i=\\{v\\in S_i:uv\\in O'(t)\\text{ for some }u\\in U\\}.\n\\]\nTheir union covers \\(U\\). The loop condition and assignment give\n\\[\n|U|\\ge6C_3|O(t)|/k.\n\\]\nChoose \\(i\\) maximizing \\(|U_i|\\); then \\(|U_i|\\ge3C_3\\).</p><p>Choose the nonavoiding record \\(B\\), with center \\(x\\), radii \\(r_1&lt;r_2\\), containing an interior ball centered at \\(u_0\\in U_i\\). Write \\(\\rho(z)=\\|X_z-x\\|_1\\), and put\n\\[\na=\\max\\{r_1,\\min_{v\\in V_i}\\rho(v)\\},\n\\quad\nb=\\min\\{r_2,\\max_{v\\in V_i}\\rho(v)\\},\n\\quad L=b-a.\n\\]\nA neighbor of \\(u_0\\) lies strictly between radii\n\\(r_1+(C_3-1)\\delta\\) and \\(r_2-(C_3-1)\\delta\\), so \\(a\\le b\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be the interior balls whose centers satisfy\n\\[\na-\\delta&lt;\\rho(u)&lt;b+\\delta,\n\\]\nand let \\(m=|\\mathcal A|\\). It is nonempty. Every ball in \\(\\mathcal A\\) is interior to \\(B\\): its radial interval makes it meet \\(B\\), including in the continuous cube. To change radius, move toward the binary center \\(x\\), or toward its opposite cube vertex; the radial change equals distance moved. If such a candidate were interior to a different record, Lemma 6.6 would be contradicted.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\nU_i\\not\\subseteq U_{\\mathcal A}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nL\\ge(C_3-1)\\delta.\n\\]\nIndeed, otherwise all \\(V_i\\) radii would lie strictly between \\(r_1,r_2\\), and every \\(U_i\\) neighbor would lie in the expanded interval. If some \\(V_i\\) radius is at least \\(r_2\\), use the neighbor of \\(u_0\\) to get \\(b=r_2\\) and \\(a&lt;r_2-(C_3-1)\\delta\\); the lower-boundary case is analogous.</p><p>**Operation 2: \\(m\\delta&gt;3L\\).** Replace \\(B\\) by\n\\[\nB_1=B(x;r_1,a-2\\delta),\\qquad\nB_2=B(x;b+2\\delta,r_2),\n\\]\nretaining its labels, and insert all balls of \\(\\mathcal A\\). Treat them. Their supports avoid the residual shells, and Lemmas 6.4, 6.6, and 6.7 prove validity.</p><p>If \\(U_i\\subseteq U_{\\mathcal A}\\), then \\(m\\ge3C_3\\). Otherwise the radial estimate gives \\(m\\ge3(C_3-1)\\). With \\(R=r_2-r_1\\), allocate to \\(B\\) both residual potentials and three quarters of every inserted ball's potential. Their sum is at least\n\\[\nR-L-4\\delta-2C_4\\delta+\\frac58m\\delta\n\\ge\n\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B)-(C_4+4)\\delta+\\frac7{24}m\\delta\n\\ge\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B).\n\\]\nEach ball retains at least \\(5\\delta/24\\ge\\theta\\delta\\) for \\(t\\).</p><p>**Operation 3: \\(m\\delta\\le3L\\).** The preceding radial estimate gives\n\\[\nL\\ge(C_3-1)\\delta;\n\\]\nif \\(U_i\\subseteq U_{\\mathcal A}\\), use\n\\(L\\ge m\\delta/3\\ge|U_i|\\delta/3\\ge C_3\\delta\\).</p><p>Replace \\(B\\) by residual shells\n\\[\nB_1=B(x;r_1,a),\\qquad B_2=B(x;b,r_2)\n\\]\nwith its labels, and central shells\n\\[\nB_3=B(x;a+\\delta,b-\\delta),\\qquad\nB_4=B(x;a,b).\n\\]\nMake \\(B_3\\) nonavoiding with owner \\(t\\) and excluded labels\n\\[\nt_P(B_3)=\\{v\\in t_P(B):v\\text{ is a proper descendant of }t\\}.\n\\]\nMake \\(B_4\\) avoiding, with owner \\(t(B)\\), avoided node \\(t\\), and \\(t_P(B_4)=t_P(B)\\). Treat all balls of \\(\\mathcal A\\), without inserting them.</p><p>Lemma 6.5 gives \\(t\\in C(B)\\) and \\(t(B)\\) a weak ancestor of \\(t\\). In fact \\(t(B)\\ne t\\). An original ball of owner \\(t\\) cannot meet another current-family ball. A synthetic record of owner \\(t\\) could only have arisen in an earlier occurrence of this operation for this same bag; that occurrence treated every remaining interior ball meeting its central shell. Subsequent restrictions and Lemma 6.8 prevent a new untreated interior ball there. Thus the avoided node is a strict descendant.</p><p>The new domains satisfy\n\\[\nC(B_3)=C(B)\\cap\\mathcal T[t],\n\\qquad\nC(B_4)=C(B)\\setminus\\mathcal T[t].\n\\]\nThey are subsets of the old domain, and all shells lie in \\(B\\). Lemma 6.7 proves compatibility with other records. The central pair has disjoint domains, and residual shells are disjoint from central shells. Excluded endpoint conditions follow from the old condition.</p><p>For crossing paths, choose \\(v_1,v_2\\in V_i\\) minimizing and maximizing \\(\\rho\\), with respective neighbors \\(u_1,u_2\\in U_i\\). Then\n\\[\n\\rho(v_1)\\le a,\\quad\\rho(v_2)\\ge b,\\quad\n\\rho(u_1)&lt;a+\\delta,\\quad\\rho(u_2)&gt;b-\\delta.\n\\]\nLocal connectivity supplies \\(k\\) edge-disjoint \\(u_1\\)-\\(u_2\\) paths in \\(G[V(t)]\\), crossing \\(B_3\\). The starting vertex avoids excluded subtrees: otherwise its edge to \\(v_1\\notin V(t)\\) belongs to a forbidden \\(P(z)\\), contradicting \\(\\rho(u_1)&lt;r_2\\).</p><p>The induced graph on \\(S_i\\) supplies at least \\(k/4\\) edge-disjoint \\(v_1\\)-\\(v_2\\) paths by Lemma 6.1. They lie in\n\\[\nV(t(B))\\setminus V(t)\n\\]\nand cross \\(B_4\\). If \\(v_1\\) belonged to an excluded \\(V(z)\\), its edge to \\(u_1\\) and the old endpoint condition would force \\(u_1\\in V(z)\\). Laminarity would make \\(z\\) a weak ancestor of \\(t\\), contrary to \\(t\\in C(B)\\). Thus \\(\\mathrm{L3}\\) holds.</p><p>Every still-untreated interior ball is disjoint from the central shells, since its center is outside \\((a-\\delta,b+\\delta)\\). This verifies the property used to exclude owner \\(t\\).</p><p>Allocate to \\(B\\) all potentials of \\(B_1,B_2,B_3\\) and \\((2C_4+2)\\delta\\) from \\(B_4\\). The first three formal widths sum to \\(R-2\\delta\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^3\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B_j)+(2C_4+2)\\delta\n\\ge R-C_4\\delta=\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B).\n\\]\nThe remaining potential of \\(B_4\\) is at least\n\\[\n\\frac{L-\\{C_4+4(C_4+2)^2\\}\\delta}{2(C_4+2)}\n\\ge\n\\frac{L-(C_3-1)\\delta/2}{2(C_4+2)}\n\\ge\\frac{L}{4(C_4+2)}\n\\ge\\frac{m\\delta}{12(C_4+2)}\n\\ge\\theta m\\delta.\n\\]\nGive this to \\(t\\).</p><p>Every operation treats at least one ball, preserves all prior allocations, and gives \\(\\theta\\delta\\) per treated ball. Border transfers do not change the treated count. The loop terminates after at most \\(|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|\\) operations, with the required strict half-bag bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.10", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cost of shrinking — paper Lemma B.22.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Cost of shrinking — paper Lemma B.22.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arrangement and potentials of Proposition 6 and Lemma 6.9.</p><p class=\"li\">At the end of phase \\(\\ell\\), let \\(Z\\) be the valid current collection, \\(p(B)=\\operatorname{tok}_\\ell(B)\\), and \\(q(B)\\) its token value at the beginning of phase \\(\\ell+1\\) without changing its support.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  b=\\sum_{t\\in T_\\ell}|\\mathrm{Bor}_t|,\n  \\qquad\n  e=\\sum_{B\\in Z}(q(B)-p(B)),\n  \\]\n  and assume\n  \\[\n  e&lt;\\frac{C_4}{6C_3}b\\delta_\\ell.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Current-phase original balls remain unchanged. Every other record of radii \\(r_1&lt;r_2\\) is restricted to\n  \\[\n  S(B)=B(x;r_1+(C_3+1)\\delta_\\ell,\\,\n                 r_2-(C_3+1)\\delta_\\ell),\n  \\]\n  discarded if its new width is nonpositive.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every deficit is nonnegative, and\n\\[\n\\mathsf D=\n\\sum_{B\\in Z}\\bigl(p(B)-q(S(B))\\bigr)\n\\le\\frac12b\\delta_\\ell.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For unchanged originals, both the deficit and \\(q(B)-p(B)\\) are zero. For another record, width decreases by \\(2(C_3+1)\\delta_\\ell\\), unless it disappears. This exceeds the threshold decrease, so the deficit is nonnegative.</p><p>Record by record,\n\\[\nq(B)-p(B)\\ge\n\\frac{C_4}{3C_3}\\bigl(p(B)-q(S(B))\\bigr).\n\\]\nIf \\(p(B)=0\\), the deficit is zero. Otherwise, for a nonavoiding record of width \\(w\\),\n\\[\nq(B)-p(B)=C_4(\\delta_\\ell-\\delta_{\\ell+1})\n\\ge\\frac23C_4\\delta_\\ell,\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\np(B)-q(S(B))\n\\le2(C_3+1)\\delta_\\ell-C_4(\\delta_\\ell-\\delta_{\\ell+1})\n\\le2C_3\\delta_\\ell.\n\\]\nFor avoiding records, both estimates have the common factor \\(1/(2(C_4+2))\\). Summing gives\n\\[\n\\mathsf D\\le(3C_3/C_4)e\\le b\\delta_\\ell/2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.11", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Arrangement of insertable bags — paper Lemma B.15.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Arrangement of insertable bags — paper Lemma B.15.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the locally \\(k\\)-connected hierarchy, selected-node boundary ratios, and constants of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">In place of its original assignment hypothesis, assume the families are \\(12C_3/k\\)-assigned and every input ball is insertable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite valid collection \\(Z\\), with centers inherited from input balls, such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{B\\in Z}\\operatorname{width}(B)\n\\ge\n\\frac{C_4}{12C_3}\n\\sum_i\\delta_i\\sum_{t\\in T_i}|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with an empty arrangement. Apply Lemma 6.9 in phase \\(\\ell\\). At its end, every bag has\n\\[\n|\\mathrm{Int}_t|&lt;|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|/2.\n\\]\nIt suffices to give each current node an additional\n\\[\n\\frac{C_4}{6C_3}|\\mathrm{Bor}_t|\\delta_\\ell.\n\\]\nTogether with its treated-ball credit, this gives at least\n\\[\n\\frac{C_4}{6C_3}\n\\bigl(|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|-|\\mathrm{Int}_t|\\bigr)\\delta_\\ell\n\\ge\n\\frac{C_4}{12C_3}|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|\\delta_\\ell.\n\\]</p><p>Define \\(p,q,b,e\\) as in Lemma 6.10. One always has \\(q(B)\\ge p(B)\\). If \\(b=0\\), no border credit is needed. If\n\\[\ne\\ge\\frac{C_4}{6C_3}b\\delta_\\ell,\n\\]\nretain the arrangement and distribute the extra tokens proportionally to border counts.</p><p>Otherwise make the restrictions \\(S(B)\\) of Lemma 6.10 and insert all border balls. A border ball whose domain meets the old record's domain satisfies\n\\[\n\\|X_u-x\\|_1\\le r_1+C_3\\delta_\\ell\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\|X_u-x\\|_1\\ge r_2-C_3\\delta_\\ell,\n\\]\nby Lemma 6.8. It therefore avoids the restricted support. When domains are disjoint, no separation is needed. Current-family balls are mutually disjoint. Lemmas 6.4 and 6.7 establish the remaining validity conditions.</p><p>Each border ball has \\(\\delta_\\ell-C_4\\delta_{\\ell+1}\\) tokens. Pool at most \\(\\delta_\\ell/2\\) from each to cover all old deficits, possible by Lemma 6.10. Restore the old allocations using this pool and the restricted-record tokens. Each border ball retains at least\n\\[\n\\delta_\\ell-C_4\\delta_{\\ell+1}-\\delta_\\ell/2\n\\ge\\delta_\\ell/3\n\\ge\\frac{C_4}{6C_3}\\delta_\\ell\n\\]\nfor its node.</p><p>Thus every phase preserves earlier credits and gives the required half-\\(\\theta\\) allocation to current nodes. With \\(\\delta_{m+1}=0\\), final potentials are at most widths, so\n\\[\n\\frac{C_4}{12C_3}\n\\sum_i\\delta_i\\sum_{t\\in T_i}|\\mathrm{Bag}_t|\n\\le\n\\sum_{B\\in Z}\\operatorname{tok}_{m+1}(B)\n\\le\n\\sum_{B\\in Z}\\operatorname{width}(B).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Polynomial shortcut bound — paper Theorem 3.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Polynomial shortcut bound — paper Theorem 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(H\\) has a hierarchy as in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">The hierarchy is locally \\(q\\)-connected: \\(H[A]\\) is \\(q\\)-edge-connected for every hierarchy node \\(A\\), with singleton conditions vacuous.</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonroot node \\(A\\), set\n  \\[\n  P(A)=\\delta_H(A),\\qquad\n  O(A)=E_H(A,A^*\\setminus A).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal M\\) is any collection of nonroot nodes satisfying\n  \\[\n  |O(A)|\\ge|P(A)|/q.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A shortcut matrix is a full symmetric positive definite matrix \\(D\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}D\\mathbf1_S\\le|\\delta_H(S)|\n  \\qquad(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V).\n  \\]\n  No constraint is imposed at \\(S=V\\), and \\(D\\preceq L_H\\) is not required.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute integer \\(q_H\\ge2\\) such that, for every integer \\(q\\ge q_H\\), \\(H\\) has a shortcut matrix \\(D\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac1{|O(A)|}\\sum_{e\\in O(A)}R_D(e)\n\\le q^{-31/40}\n\\qquad(A\\in\\mathcal M).\n\\]\nThe cutoff is independent of the graph, hierarchy depth, and marked collection.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\mathcal M=\\varnothing\\), a sufficiently small positive multiple of the identity works. Otherwise fix any binary embedding \\(X\\) and row semiorthogonal \\(U\\) in Proposition 2, with\n\\[\n\\mathcal E=\\sum_e\\|Xb_e\\|_2^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nChange row signs so that\n\\[\na_e=\\langle u_e,Xb_e\\rangle\\ge0.\n\\]\nPut \\(d_e=\\|Xb_e\\|_2^2=\\|Xb_e\\|_1\\), and\n\\[\nN_t=\\frac1{|O(t)|}\\left(\\sum_{e\\in O(t)}a_e\\right)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n\\alpha=q^{-4/5},\\quad\n\\varepsilon=1/10,\\quad\n\\beta=36,\\quad\n\\rho=\\frac1{2q^2},\\quad\nC_4=3,\\quad C_3=10^4.\n\\]\nLet \\(T\\subseteq\\mathcal M\\) be the \\(\\alpha\\)-bad nodes with positive numerator. Every edge belongs to at most two \\(O(t)\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal M\\setminus T}N_t\\le2\\alpha\\mathcal E.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(T\\ne\\varnothing\\), apply Proposition 4 with geometric ratio \\(\\rho\\). Its parameters are\n\\[\nJ=1+\\lceil\\log_2(256/\\alpha^2)\\rceil,\\quad\nC_2=128J^4,\\quad s=\\alpha/C_2,\n\\]\n\\[\nL=2+\\lceil\\log_2(1/s)\\rceil,\\quad\nH=1+\\lceil\\log_2(24/(\\rho s^2))\\rceil,\\quad\nC_1=1024\\varepsilon^{-2}.\n\\]\nFor absolute constants and sufficiently large \\(q\\),\n\\[\nC_2\\le c_2(1+\\log q)^4,\\qquad\nL,H\\le c_5(1+\\log q).\n\\]\nThe smallness condition holds eventually because \\(s^\\varepsilon\\le q^{-2/25}\\to0\\). Assignment strength also satisfies\n\\[\ns^{1+2\\varepsilon}\n=\\frac{q^{-24/25}}{C_2^{6/5}}\n\\ge\\frac{24C_3}{q}\n\\]\neventually, since \\(q^{1/25}/C_2^{6/5}\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>The resulting sequence has mass\n\\[\n\\mathsf M\\ge\n\\frac{s^\\varepsilon}{384\\beta C_1C_2LH}\n\\sum_{t\\in T}N_t.\n\\]\nIn the assigned case,\n\\[\n|O(t)|\\ge q^{-1}|P(t)|\\ge q\\rho|P(t)|,\n\\]\nso Proposition 6 applies with \\(k=q\\), \\(\\lambda=\\rho\\). In the compact case Proposition 5 applies. Both give\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\ge\\kappa q\\mathsf M,\n\\qquad\n\\kappa=\\frac{C_4}{96C_3}=\\frac1{320000}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sum_{t\\in T}N_t}{\\mathcal E}\n\\le\n\\frac{384\\beta C_1C_2LH}{\\kappa q s^\\varepsilon}\n\\le c_6q^{-23/25}(1+\\log q)^7.\n\\]\nThe logarithmic power before rounding is \\(4(11/10)+2=32/5&lt;7\\).</p><p>Including \\(T=\\varnothing\\), every dual ratio is therefore at most\n\\[\n2q^{-4/5}+c_6q^{-23/25}(1+\\log q)^7\n=o(q^{-31/40}).\n\\]\nIncrease the absolute cutoff so this is at most \\(q^{-31/40}/2\\), and so all preceding numerical conditions hold. Proposition 2 with \\(K=\\eta=q^{-31/40}/2\\) supplies a feasible full positive definite \\(D\\) whose marked averages are less than \\(q^{-31/40}\\). No attainment of the infimum is asserted or needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite-dimensional fixed point — paper Theorem C.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Finite-dimensional fixed point — paper Theorem C.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\) is a nonempty compact convex subset of a finite-dimensional real vector space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F:K\\to K\\) is continuous.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(x\\in K\\) with \\(F(x)=x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First let \\(F:\\Delta_d\\to\\Delta_d\\). If a subdivision vertex is fixed, the result follows. Otherwise label each vertex \\(v\\) by an index \\(i\\) with \\(v_i&gt;F_i(v)\\). Such an index exists because coordinate sums agree, and it has \\(v_i&gt;0\\).</p><p>Lemmas 8.1 and 8.2 supply fully labeled simplices with mesh tending to zero. Write their vertices as \\(v^{(m,i)}\\), labeled \\(i\\). A subsequence of \\(v^{(m,0)}\\) converges to \\(x\\); all other vertices converge to the same \\(x\\). Continuity gives \\(x_i\\ge F_i(x)\\) for every \\(i\\), and equality of coordinate sums forces equality in every coordinate. Affine coordinates give the result for any simplex.</p><p>Equip the affine hull of \\(K\\) with Euclidean coordinates. Each \\(x\\) has a unique nearest point \\(P(x)\\in K\\). Existence follows from compactness. Uniqueness follows from convexity and\n\\[\n\\left\\|x-\\frac{p+q}{2}\\right\\|^2\n=\n\\frac{\\|x-p\\|^2+\\|x-q\\|^2}{2}\n-\\frac{\\|p-q\\|^2}{4}.\n\\]\nFor \\(p=P(x)\\), comparison with \\(p+t(z-p)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\langle x-p,z-p\\rangle\\le0\\qquad(z\\in K).\n\\]\nApply this with \\(z=P(y)=q\\), and the analogous inequality for \\(y,q,p\\). Adding yields\n\\[\n\\|p-q\\|^2\\le\\langle x-y,p-q\\rangle\n\\le\\|x-y\\|\\|p-q\\|.\n\\]\nThus \\(P\\) is continuous and fixes \\(K\\).</p><p>If the affine hull has dimension zero, the result is immediate. Otherwise identify it with \\(\\mathbb R^d\\), choose \\(M&gt;0\\) with \\(K\\subseteq[-M,M]^d\\), and use the containing simplex\n\\[\nS=\\{x:x_j\\ge-M,\\ \\sum_jx_j\\le dM\\}.\n\\]\nIts vertices are \\(a=(-M,\\ldots,-M)\\) and \\(a+2dMe_j\\). The simplex result applied to \\(F\\circ P:S\\to K\\subseteq S\\) gives a fixed point \\(x\\). Its image lies in \\(K\\), so \\(P(x)=x\\) and \\(F(x)=x\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Barycentric subdivisions used in Appendix C.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Barycentric subdivisions used in Appendix C.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(S\\) is a \\(d\\)-simplex with vertices \\(a_0,\\ldots,a_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonempty subset \\(A\\) of its vertices, write\n  \\[\n  b_A=|A|^{-1}\\sum_{a\\in A}a.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The simplices\n\\[\n\\operatorname{conv}\\{b_{A_0},\\ldots,b_{A_d}\\},\n\\qquad\nA_k=\\{a_{\\pi(0)},\\ldots,a_{\\pi(k)}\\},\n\\]\nover all permutations \\(\\pi\\), together with their faces, form a finite triangulation. The construction agrees on shared faces. Repeated subdivision has mesh tending to zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>The barycenters in a chain are affinely independent because their coordinate columns form an invertible triangular matrix.</p><p>For \\(x=\\sum_i\\lambda_ia_i\\), order the coordinates by\n\\(\\lambda_{\\pi(0)}\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_{\\pi(d)}\\), put\n\\(\\lambda_{\\pi(d+1)}=0\\), and define\n\\[\nt_k=(k+1)(\\lambda_{\\pi(k)}-\\lambda_{\\pi(k+1)}).\n\\]\nThen \\(t_k\\ge0\\), \\(\\sum_kt_k=1\\), and\n\\[\nx=\\sum_kt_kb_{A_k}.\n\\]\nConversely, combinations of these barycenters have coordinates in that order. Whenever \\(t_k&gt;0\\), \\(A_k\\) is determined by the corresponding positive coordinate level of \\(x\\), independently of tied-coordinate order. Thus simplices intersect exactly in the convex hull of common vertices.</p><p>Coordinates outside a face vanish, so subdivision restricts to subdivision on that face. For \\(A\\subsetneq B\\),\n\\[\nb_B=\\frac{|A|}{|B|}b_A+\n\\left(1-\\frac{|A|}{|B|}\\right)b_{B\\setminus A},\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\n\\|b_B-b_A\\|\\le\\frac d{d+1}\\operatorname{diam}(S).\n\\]\nThe diameter of a convex hull is at most the largest vertex distance. Repeated subdivision therefore contracts mesh by \\(d/(d+1)\\) for \\(d\\ge1\\); \\(d=0\\) is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Sperner's counting argument — paper Lemma C.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Sperner's counting argument — paper Lemma C.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Delta_d=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{d+1}:x_i\\ge0,\\ \\sum_{i=0}^dx_i=1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A finite triangulation has vertices labeled \\(\\ell(v)\\in\\{0,\\ldots,d\\}\\), with \\(v_{\\ell(v)}&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The number of \\(d\\)-simplices whose labels are \\(0,\\ldots,d\\), each once, is odd.</p>", "proof": "<p>A small \\((d-1)\\)-face belongs to two small \\(d\\)-simplices if not in the boundary and to one if in the boundary. To verify this, choose a relative interior point. By the common-face property, every simplex containing it contains the entire face. A sufficiently small ball avoids all other simplices. Each incident full-dimensional simplex occupies one side of the face's hyperplane near the point; disjoint interiors allow at most one on either side, and covering supplies every side inside \\(\\Delta_d\\). A boundary \\((d-1)\\)-face lies in exactly one original facet, since distinct facets meet in dimension at most \\(d-2\\).</p><p>Induct on \\(d\\). The case \\(d=0\\) has one vertex. Call a small \\((d-1)\\)-face special if its labels are exactly \\(0,\\ldots,d-1\\). A fully labeled \\(d\\)-simplex contributes one incidence with special faces. A simplex containing \\(0,\\ldots,d-1\\) but not \\(d\\) has one repeated label and contributes two. All others contribute zero.</p><p>Modulo two, the incidence count is both the number of fully labeled simplices and the number of special boundary faces. A special boundary face cannot lie in \\(x_i=0\\) for \\(i&lt;d\\). Thus these are exactly the fully labeled simplices of the induced triangulation of \\(x_d=0\\). Induction makes their number odd.</p><p>The induced triangulation exists because intersection with a coordinate hyperplane selects exactly the vertices on it. Full-dimensional face members cover the original face; finitely many lower-dimensional members cannot cover a relatively open subset. Every smaller member is a face of a full-dimensional one by the common-face property.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Shortcut extraction — paper Theorem 4.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Shortcut extraction — paper Theorem 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(H\\) is an \\(r\\)-tree-packable graph on \\(n\\ge2\\) vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  u=r^{-1/16},\\qquad q=\\lfloor(1-u)r\\rfloor.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large integers \\(r\\), there is \\(X\\succ0\\) such that\n\\[\nL_H\\preceq X,\\qquad\n\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}X\\mathbf1_S\\le3|\\delta_H(S)|\n\\quad(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V),\n\\]\nand the edges satisfying\n\\[\nR_X(e)\\le4r^{-1/2}\n\\]\ncontain \\(q\\) edge-disjoint spanning trees. The lower threshold on \\(r\\) is absolute.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each positive integer \\(p\\), cover \\(\\mathcal K\\) by finitely many relative open balls of radius \\(1/p\\), centered at \\(C_{p,j}\\in\\mathcal K\\). Choose a response \\(Y_{p,j}\\) from Lemma 9.2 at each center. Define\n\\[\n\\phi_{p,j}(Z)=\n\\frac{\\max\\{0,p^{-1}-\\|Z-C_{p,j}\\|\\}}\n{\\sum_h\\max\\{0,p^{-1}-\\|Z-C_{p,h}\\|\\}},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Phi_p(Z)=\\sum_j\\phi_{p,j}(Z)Y_{p,j}.\n\\]\nThe denominator is positive. This is a continuous self-map of \\(\\mathcal K\\), so Proposition 8 gives \\(X_p=\\Phi_p(X_p)\\). Pass to a subsequence with \\(X_p\\to X\\in\\mathcal K\\).</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n3r^{-1/2}&lt;s&lt;4r^{-1/2}\n\\]\ndifferent from every \\(R_X(e)\\). If \\(\\phi_{p,j}(X_p)&gt;0\\), then\n\\[\n\\|C_{p,j}-X\\|&lt;p^{-1}+\\|X_p-X\\|\\to0\n\\]\nuniformly. Resistance is continuous on \\(\\mathcal K\\); thus, for large \\(p\\), all contributing centers have the same threshold graph, packing partition, and crossing set as \\(X\\).</p><p>Write \\(B=B_s(X)\\), \\(M=|B|\\), and \\(\\delta=C_0r^{-1/4}\\). If \\(M=0\\), connectedness of \\(H\\) forces the partition to be \\(\\{V\\}\\). Otherwise every contributing response has at most \\(\\delta M\\) bad edges in this same \\(B\\). Weighted summation shows that at most \\(2\\delta M\\) edges receive bad weight greater than one half.</p><p>For each remaining edge, at least half the weight is on matrices satisfying\n\\[\nY_{p,j}\\succeq r^{1/2}b_eb_e^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\nby Proposition 1. The remaining matrices are positive semidefinite, so\n\\[\nX_p\\succeq\\tfrac12r^{1/2}b_eb_e^{\\mathsf T},\n\\qquad R_{X_p}(e)\\le2r^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nAt least \\(\\lceil(1-2\\delta)M\\rceil\\) edges satisfy this closed inequality. This count condition is a finite union of finite intersections of closed conditions, so it persists at \\(X\\). These edges belong to \\(F_s(X)\\).</p><p>If the partition has \\(d&gt;1\\) parts, the original \\(r\\) trees give \\(M\\ge r(d-1)\\). Choose the absolute cutoff so that \\(2C_0r^{-1/4}&lt;u\\). Then\n\\[\n|E_{F_s(X)}(\\mathcal P_s(X))|\n\\ge(1-2C_0r^{-1/4})M\n&gt;q(d-1),\n\\]\ncontradicting Proposition 1's maximal-partition bound. Thus the partition is \\(\\{V\\}\\), and \\(F_s(X)\\) contains \\(q\\) disjoint spanning trees. Their resistances are below \\(4r^{-1/2}\\), and \\(X\\in\\mathcal K\\) gives the matrix bounds.</p><p>The finite-cover scale needed to stabilize a threshold may depend arbitrarily on the fixed graph. The absolute cutoff on \\(r\\) comes only from the hierarchy estimate and numerical inequalities; no computational-cost claim is made.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Compact matrix domain and hierarchy response — paper Sections 4.1–4.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Compact matrix domain and hierarchy response — paper Sections 4.1–4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(H,n,r,u,q\\) from Proposition 9, with \\(r\\) above a sufficiently large absolute threshold.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal K=\n  \\left\\{\n  X=X^{\\mathsf T}:\n  X\\succeq L_H+n^{-1}I,\\ \n  \\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}X\\mathbf1_S\\le3|\\delta_H(S)|\n  \\text{ for }\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(\\mathcal K\\) is nonempty, compact, and convex, and every member is at least \\(n^{-1}I\\). For any locally \\(q\\)-connected hierarchy, mark nodes satisfying \\(|O(A)|\\ge|P(A)|/q\\). There exists \\(Y\\in\\mathcal K\\) such that\n\\[\n\\frac1{|O(A)|}\\sum_{e\\in O(A)}R_Y(e)\\le r^{-3/4}\n\\]\nfor every marked node.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(H\\) is connected,\n\\[\n|S|/n&lt;1\\le|\\delta_H(S)|,\n\\]\nso \\(L_H+n^{-1}I\\in\\mathcal K\\). Defining inequalities give closedness and convexity. Singleton cuts give \\(X_{vv}\\le3\\deg_H(v)\\), and positive semidefiniteness gives\n\\[\n|X_{vw}|^2\\le X_{vv}X_{ww}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal K\\) is bounded.</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(r\\), \\(q\\ge r/2\\) and \\(q\\ge q_H\\). Proposition 7 gives a shortcut \\(D\\) with marked averages at most\n\\[\nq^{-31/40}\\le2^{31/40}r^{-31/40}\\le r^{-3/4},\n\\]\nthe last inequality holding for \\(r\\ge2^{31}\\). Put\n\\[\nY=L_H+n^{-1}I+D.\n\\]\nIts cut quadratic forms are at most \\(2|\\delta_H(S)|+|S|/n\\le3|\\delta_H(S)|\\), so \\(Y\\in\\mathcal K\\). Proposition 1's inverse monotonicity gives the asserted resistance averages.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Simultaneous response — paper Lemma 4.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous response — paper Lemma 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 9 and the compact domain \\(\\mathcal K\\) of Lemma 9.1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(X\\in\\mathcal K\\), \\(s&gt;0\\), define\n  \\[\n  F_s(X)=(V,\\{e\\in E(H):R_X(e)\\le s\\}),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal P_s(X)=\\mathcal P(F_s(X)),\\quad\n  B_s(X)=E_H(\\mathcal P_s(X)),\\quad M_s(X)=|B_s(X)|,\n  \\]\n  where maximal packable parts use parameter \\(q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute \\(C_0\\), which may be \\(40\\), such that every \\(X\\in\\mathcal K\\) has a response \\(Y\\in\\mathcal K\\) with\n\\[\n|\\{e\\in B_s(X):R_Y(e)&gt;r^{-1/2}\\}|\n\\le C_0r^{-1/4}M_s(X)\n\\qquad(s&gt;0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppress \\(X\\). As \\(s\\) increases, the partitions coarsen and crossing sets decrease. Each part induces a \\(q\\)-edge-connected subgraph of \\(H\\), since it contains \\(q\\) packed trees.</p><p>In every occupied bin \\(2^a\\le M_s&lt;2^{a+1}\\), choose the finest occurring partition. List these from fine to coarse as\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_0,\\ldots,\\mathcal P_{\\ell-1},\n\\]\nwith crossing sets \\(B_i\\), sizes \\(M_i\\), and append \\(\\mathcal P_\\ell=\\{V\\}\\), \\(M_\\ell=0\\). Distinct parts of these partitions, together with all singletons, form a hierarchy. Each node induces a \\(q\\)-edge-connected graph. Take its response \\(Y\\) from Lemma 9.1.</p><p>At a transition \\(\\mathcal P_j\\to\\mathcal P_{j+1}\\), every changing part has as immediate parent its containing part of \\(\\mathcal P_{j+1}\\). An edge disappearing from \\(B_j\\) belongs to the \\(O(A)\\) sets of its two changing parts. The sum of full boundary sizes of changing parts is at most \\(2M_j\\).</p><p>Unmarked changing parts have total \\(|O(A)|\\) at most \\(2M_j/q\\). For marked parts, the average bound and Markov's inequality give at most\n\\[\nr^{-1/4}|O(A)|\n\\]\nedges with \\(R_Y(e)&gt;r^{-1/2}\\). Thus at most\n\\[\n2(r^{-1/4}+1/q)M_j\n\\]\ndisappearing edges are bad.</p><p>There is one representative per occupied dyadic bin, so\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=i}^{\\ell-1}M_j&lt;4M_i.\n\\]\nEvery edge of \\(B_i\\) disappears exactly once, giving at most\n\\[\n8(r^{-1/4}+1/q)M_i\n\\]\nbad edges in \\(B_i\\).</p><p>For arbitrary \\(s\\) with \\(M_s&gt;0\\), its bin representative is finer, so \\(B_s\\subseteq B_i\\) and \\(M_i&lt;2M_s\\). Its bad-edge count is therefore at most\n\\[\n16(r^{-1/4}+1/q)M_s\\le40r^{-1/4}M_s\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(r\\). The zero case is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Rank-preserving isotropic representation and packing certification — paper Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Rank-preserving isotropic representation and packing certification — paper Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\) is the union of \\(q\\ge1\\) edge-disjoint spanning trees on \\(n\\ge2\\) vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Edges are individually indexed, including parallel edges.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\rho&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are vectors \\(z_e\\in\\mathbb R^{n-1}\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_{e\\in B}z_ez_e^{\\mathsf T}=I_{n-1},\n\\qquad\n\\|z_e\\|^2\\le\\frac{1+\\rho}{q},\n\\]\nof the form\n\\[\nz_e=s_eMb_e,\n\\]\nwhere \\(s_e&gt;0\\) and \\(M:\\mathbf1^\\perp\\to\\mathbb R^{n-1}\\) is invertible. Thus every subset has the same rank in the \\(z_e\\) representation as in the incidence representation.</p><p>For these vectors, if \\(A\\subseteq E(B)\\), \\(c&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{e\\in A}z_ez_e^{\\mathsf T}\\succeq cI_{n-1},\n\\]\nthen \\((V,A)\\) contains at least\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{cq}{1+\\rho}\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\nedge-disjoint spanning trees.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 10.1 constructs the representation, and Lemma 10.2 gives its packing certificate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Isotropic representation preserving ranks — paper Lemma 5.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Isotropic representation preserving ranks — paper Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(B,q,n,\\rho\\) are as in Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The vectors and positive scalings in the first assertion of Proposition 10 exist.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose orthonormal coordinates on \\(\\mathbf1^\\perp\\), and write \\(b_e\\) for incidence columns in these coordinates. Let the disjoint trees be \\(T_1,\\ldots,T_q\\). For \\(a\\in\\mathbb R^{E(B)}\\), put\n\\[\nN(a)=\\sum_{e\\in B}e^{a_e}b_eb_e^{\\mathsf T},\n\\qquad\nh(a)=\\log\\det N(a)-\\frac1q\\sum_{e\\in B}a_e.\n\\]\nEach \\(N(a)\\) is positive definite by Proposition 1's incidence-rank assertion.</p><p>Let \\(c_t&gt;0\\) be the squared determinant of the columns of \\(T_t\\). Cauchy–Binet and arithmetic–geometric mean give\n\\[\n\\det N(a)\n\\ge\\sum_{t=1}^qc_t\\exp\\left(\\sum_{e\\in T_t}a_e\\right)\n\\ge\nq\\left(\\prod_tc_t\\right)^{1/q}\n\\exp\\left(\\frac1q\\sum_{e\\in B}a_e\\right).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nh(a)\\ge L:=\\log q+\\frac1q\\sum_t\\log c_t.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), the coercive function \\(h(a)+\\gamma\\|a\\|^2\\) has a minimizer \\(a^\\gamma\\). With \\(K=h(0)-L\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\gamma\\|a^\\gamma\\|^2\\le K,\n\\qquad\n\\|\\nabla h(a^\\gamma)\\|\n=2\\gamma\\|a^\\gamma\\|\n\\le2\\sqrt{\\gamma K}.\n\\]\nChoose a finite \\(a\\) with each gradient coordinate of absolute value at most \\(\\rho/q\\). This includes \\(K=0\\).</p><p>The determinant derivative formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial a_e}\n=e^{a_e}b_e^{\\mathsf T}N(a)^{-1}b_e-\\frac1q.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nz_e=e^{a_e/2}N(a)^{-1/2}b_e.\n\\]\nTheir outer products sum to the identity, and their squared norms are at most \\((1+\\rho)/q\\). The common map is invertible and all scalars are positive, so every subset rank is preserved.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Projected trace and packing — paper Lemma 5.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Projected trace and packing — paper Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the vectors of Lemma 10.1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\subseteq E(B)\\), \\(c&gt;0\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{e\\in A}z_ez_e^{\\mathsf T}\\succeq cI_{n-1}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\((V,A)\\) contains at least \\(\\lfloor cq/(1+\\rho)\\rfloor\\) edge-disjoint spanning trees.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a partition \\(\\mathcal P=\\{V_1,\\ldots,V_d\\}\\). Let \\(W\\) span all \\(z_e\\) of \\(B\\) with both endpoints in one part. Rank preservation gives\n\\[\n\\dim W\\le\\sum_j(|V_j|-1)=n-d.\n\\]\nLet \\(P\\) be orthogonal projection onto \\(W^\\perp\\), so \\(\\operatorname{tr}P\\ge d-1\\). Taking projected traces,\n\\[\nc(d-1)\\le c\\operatorname{tr}P\n\\le\n\\operatorname{tr}\\left(P\\sum_{e\\in A}z_ez_e^{\\mathsf T}\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{e\\in E_A(\\mathcal P)}\\|Pz_e\\|^2\n\\le\\frac{1+\\rho}{q}|E_A(\\mathcal P)|.\n\\]\nWithin-part terms vanish. Proposition 1's partition criterion proves the assertion; if the floor is zero, it is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Isotropic random-vector theorem — paper Theorem A.1, Marcus–Spielman–Srivastava.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Isotropic random-vector theorem — paper Theorem A.1, Marcus–Spielman–Srivastava.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(v_1,\\ldots,v_m\\) are independent finitely supported random vectors in \\(\\mathbb C^d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The symbol \\(^*\\) denotes conjugate transpose, and \\(\\|\\cdot\\|\\) is operator norm for matrices.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E[v_iv_i^*]=I_d,\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathbb E\\|v_i\\|^2\\le\\varepsilon.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb P\\left(\n\\left\\|\\sum_{i=1}^mv_iv_i^*\\right\\|\n\\le(1+\\sqrt\\varepsilon)^2\n\\right)&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The case \\(d=0\\) is immediate. Otherwise let \\(A_i=\\mathbb E[v_iv_i^*]\\). These matrices satisfy\n\\[\nA_i\\succeq0,\\qquad\n\\sum_iA_i=I_d,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{tr}A_i=\\mathbb E\\|v_i\\|^2\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nLemma 11.8 bounds the largest root of their mixed polynomial. Lemma 11.5 selects a positive-probability outcome whose largest eigenvalue is no larger. Since the realized sum is positive semidefinite, that eigenvalue is its operator norm.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Limits of univariate polynomials — paper Lemma A.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Limits of univariate polynomials — paper Lemma A.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Nonzero univariate polynomials \\(f_\\nu\\) have uniformly bounded degrees and converge coefficientwise to a nonzero polynomial \\(f\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb H=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:\\operatorname{Im}z&gt;0\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If every \\(f_\\nu\\) has no zero in \\(\\mathbb H\\), neither does \\(f\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If every \\(f_\\nu\\) is real-rooted, so is \\(f\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For monic real-rooted polynomials of a fixed degree, ordered roots depend continuously on coefficients.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(a\\in\\mathbb H\\) with \\(f(a)\\ne0\\). Pass to a subsequence of constant degree and enumerate the roots \\(\\lambda_{\\nu,k}\\). Compactness of the complex plane with infinity gives a further subsequence on which all roots converge, allowing infinite limits. Every finite limit lies outside \\(\\mathbb H\\).</p><p>In\n\\[\n\\frac{f_\\nu(z)}{f_\\nu(a)}\n=\\prod_k\\frac{z-\\lambda_{\\nu,k}}{a-\\lambda_{\\nu,k}},\n\\]\na root tending to infinity contributes one in the limit; a finite limiting root \\(\\lambda_k\\) contributes \\((z-\\lambda_k)/(a-\\lambda_k)\\). Thus all roots of \\(f\\) lie outside \\(\\mathbb H\\). For real-rooted polynomials use the extended real line instead.</p><p>For monic polynomials, bounded coefficient vectors have bounded roots: a root of \\(z^d+\\sum_{k&lt;d}a_kz^k\\) has modulus at most \\(1+\\max_k|a_k|\\), by a geometric-series estimate. Any convergent subsequence of ordered root tuples factors the limiting polynomial, so its limit is the unique ordered root tuple of that polynomial.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Elementary stability operations — paper Lemma A.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Elementary stability operations — paper Lemma A.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A nonzero multivariate polynomial is stable if it does not vanish when all arguments belong to \\(\\mathbb H\\), and real stable if its coefficients are real.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(A_1,\\ldots,A_m\\) are Hermitian positive semidefinite and \\(C\\) is Hermitian, then\n   \\[\n   \\det\\left(xI+C+\\sum_iz_iA_i\\right)\n   \\]\n   is real stable. If \\(\\sum_iA_i\\succ0\\), then \\(\\det(\\sum_iz_iA_i)\\) is real stable.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(p\\) is real stable, then \\((1-\\partial_i)p\\) is real stable.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. A nonzero univariate polynomial obtained by setting all other variables of a real stable polynomial to real constants is real-rooted.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the determinant, the matrix's imaginary part is\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{Im}x)I+\\sum_i(\\operatorname{Im}z_i)A_i\\succ0.\n\\]\nA kernel vector would contradict strict positivity of the imaginary part of its quadratic form. The second determinant is handled by \\(\\sum_iA_i\\succ0\\). Real arguments give Hermitian matrices and real determinants, hence real coefficients.</p><p>For the differential operation, fix arguments in \\(\\mathbb H\\) and view \\(p\\) as a polynomial in argument \\(i\\). Its roots \\(\\alpha_k\\) have nonpositive imaginary parts. If nonconstant,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial_ip}{p}\n=\\sum_k\\frac1{z_i-\\alpha_k}\n\\]\nhas strictly negative imaginary part. If constant, the quotient is zero. It cannot equal one, so \\((1-\\partial_i)p\\ne0\\).</p><p>For specialization, replace each fixed real constant \\(a\\) by \\(a+i\\eta\\), and let \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\). Apply Lemma 11.1 to the resulting univariate polynomials.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Expectation identity and real roots — paper Lemma A.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Expectation identity and real roots — paper Lemma A.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A_1,\\ldots,A_m\\) are Hermitian positive semidefinite \\(d\\times d\\) matrices.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mu[A_1,\\ldots,A_m](x)=\n  \\left.\n  \\left(\\prod_{i=1}^m(1-\\partial_{z_i})\\right)\n  \\det\\left(xI_d+\\sum_{i=1}^mz_iA_i\\right)\n  \\right|_{z_1=\\cdots=z_m=0}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This polynomial is monic, degree \\(d\\), and real-rooted. If \\(v_i\\) are independent finitely supported vectors with \\(A_i=\\mathbb E[v_iv_i^*]\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\det\\left(xI_d-\\sum_iv_iv_i^*\\right)\n=\\mu[A_1,\\ldots,A_m](x).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 11.2 gives stability before and after the operators. The coefficient of \\(x^d\\) is one, independent of all \\(z_i\\), and terms involving any \\(z_i\\) have smaller \\(x\\)-degree. Thus specialization is nonzero and monic. Specialize all \\(z_i=i\\eta\\) and use Lemma 11.1 to obtain real-rootedness.</p><p>For a fixed matrix \\(M\\) and random vector \\(v\\), with \\(A=\\mathbb E[vv^*]\\), the rank-one determinant identity gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\det(M-vv^*)\n=\n\\det M-\\operatorname{tr}(\\operatorname{adj}(M)A)\n=\n\\left.(1-\\partial_z)\\det(M+zA)\\right|_{z=0}.\n\\]\nThese polynomial identities also hold for singular \\(M\\). Apply conditionally to \\(v_m,v_{m-1},\\ldots,v_1\\). Independence keeps covariances unchanged; finite sums, differentiation, and specialization commute.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Elementary interlacing criterion — paper Lemma A.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Elementary interlacing criterion — paper Lemma A.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A finite collection consists of monic degree-\\(d\\) polynomials.</p><p class=\"li\">Every convex combination is real-rooted.</p><p class=\"li\">For a real-rooted \\(f\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_1(f)\\le\\cdots\\le\\lambda_d(f)\n  \\]\n  for its roots with multiplicity.</p><p class=\"li\">A common interlacing is a sequence \\(c_1\\le\\cdots\\le c_{d-1}\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_k(f)\\le c_k\\le\\lambda_{k+1}(f)\n  \\]\n  for every polynomial.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The collection has a common interlacing. For any probability weights, some positive-weight member has largest root at most the largest root of the weighted average.</p>", "proof": "<p>For two members \\(p,q\\), let \\(r_t=(1-t)p+tq\\). Fix real \\(x\\) avoiding roots of \\(p,q\\), and count roots of \\(r_t\\) strictly below \\(x\\). Ordered-root continuity makes this count locally constant unless \\(r_t(x)=0\\), which occurs at most once.</p><p>At an interior crossing \\(t_0\\), the root \\(x\\) is simple. Otherwise, if its multiplicity is \\(\\ell\\ge2\\),\n\\[\nr_{t_0}(x+w)=aw^\\ell+O(w^{\\ell+1}),\n\\quad a\\ne0.\n\\]\nFor \\(h\\to0\\) of sign \\(\\sigma\\),\n\\[\n|h|^{-1}r_{t_0+h}(x+|h|^{1/\\ell}z)\n\\longrightarrow az^\\ell+\\sigma b,\n\\quad b=q(x)-p(x)\\ne0.\n\\]\nLemma 11.1 would make the limit real-rooted. For \\(\\ell=2\\), choose \\(\\sigma b/a&gt;0\\); for \\(\\ell\\ge3\\), either sign gives nonreal roots. This is impossible.</p><p>The root count therefore changes by at most one. If \\(\\lambda_k(p)&gt;\\lambda_{k+1}(q)\\), choose \\(x\\) between them to obtain a count difference at least two. Thus\n\\[\n\\lambda_k(p_i)\\le\\lambda_{k+1}(p_j)\n\\quad\\text{for all }i,j,\n\\]\nand \\(c_k=\\max_i\\lambda_k(p_i)\\) is a common interlacing.</p><p>Discard zero weights and let \\(P\\) be the weighted average, with largest root \\(\\rho\\). For \\(d=1\\), roots average. For \\(d&gt;1\\), put \\(c=c_{d-1}\\). Each \\(p_i(c)\\le0\\), hence \\(P(c)\\le0\\) and \\(\\rho\\ge c\\). For \\(x&gt;\\rho\\), \\(P(x)&gt;0\\), so some \\(p_i(x)&gt;0\\). Its first \\(d-1\\) roots are below \\(x\\), forcing its last root below \\(x\\). Let \\(x\\downarrow\\rho\\) and use finiteness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Selection from independent supports — paper Lemma A.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Selection from independent supports — paper Lemma A.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(v_1,\\ldots,v_m\\) are independent finitely supported vectors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Some positive-probability outcome has largest eigenvalue of \\(\\sum_iv_iv_i^*\\) at most the largest root of\n\\[\n\\mu[\\mathbb Ev_1v_1^*,\\ldots,\\mathbb Ev_mv_m^*].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Delete zero-probability support points. At every partial assignment, the conditional expected characteristic polynomial is monic and real-rooted by Lemma 11.3, treating fixed vectors as deterministic.</p><p>Every convex combination of its children is again an expected characteristic polynomial, with the next vector given the combination's probabilities. Lemma 11.4 permits a positive-probability child whose largest root does not increase. Repeat through all supports. The leaf has positive probability and its polynomial is the characteristic polynomial of the realized sum.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive partial fractions — paper Lemma A.7.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Positive partial fractions — paper Lemma A.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(p\\) is real stable.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Ab}_p=\n  \\{z\\in\\mathbb R^m:p(z+s)&gt;0\\text{ for every }s\\in[0,\\infty)^m\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z\\in\\operatorname{Ab}_p\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_p^i(z)=\\frac{\\partial_ip(z)}{p(z)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(i,j\\) are coordinate indices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As a rational function of \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi_p^i(z+te_j)\n=c+\\sum_{\\ell=1}^s\\frac{c_\\ell}{t-\\lambda_\\ell},\n\\qquad\nc\\ge0,\\quad c_\\ell&gt;0,\\quad\\lambda_\\ell&lt;0,\n\\]\nwith an empty sum allowed. For \\(t\\ge0\\), it is nonnegative, nonincreasing, and convex.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(h(t)=p(z+te_j)\\), \\(a(t)=\\partial_ip(z+te_j)\\), and \\(R=a/h\\). By Lemma 11.2, \\(h\\) is real-rooted, and positivity on \\([0,\\infty)\\) puts its roots strictly below zero. Thus the poles of \\(R\\), after cancellation, are negative real numbers.</p><p>For \\(t\\in\\mathbb H\\), add \\(i\\eta\\) to all other coordinates. The logarithmic-derivative calculation in Lemma 11.2 gives nonpositive imaginary part. Letting \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\) yields \\(\\operatorname{Im}R(t)\\le0\\).</p><p>For real \\(t\\ge0\\), the polynomial \\(g(s)=p(z+te_j+se_i)\\) is real-rooted and positive on \\(s\\ge0\\), so\n\\[\nR(t)=g'(0)/g(0)=\\sum_k(-\\beta_k)^{-1}\\ge0,\n\\]\nwhere the \\(\\beta_k&lt;0\\) are its roots.</p><p>At a pole \\(\\lambda\\) of order \\(b\\), a leading principal term \\(a_0(t-\\lambda)^{-b}\\) has imaginary part\n\\[\n-a_0r^{-b}\\sin(b\\theta)\n\\]\nat \\(t=\\lambda+re^{i\\theta}\\). For \\(b\\ge2\\), this changes sign in the upper half-plane. Thus poles are simple, and their residues are positive.</p><p>Subtract the principal parts. A real polynomial remainder of degree at least two likewise has imaginary parts of both signs along large upper-half-plane rays. A linear remainder must have nonpositive slope; nonnegativity on all positive real \\(t\\) rules out negative slope and forces a nonnegative constant. This proves the representation. Differentiation gives\n\\[\nR'(t)=-\\sum_\\ell\\frac{c_\\ell}{(t-\\lambda_\\ell)^2}\\le0,\n\\qquad\nR''(t)=2\\sum_\\ell\\frac{c_\\ell}{(t-\\lambda_\\ell)^3}\\ge0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**One barrier shift — paper Lemma A.8.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**One barrier shift — paper Lemma A.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(p\\) is real stable, \\(z\\in\\operatorname{Ab}_p\\), and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_p^j(z)+1/\\delta\\le1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(q=(1-\\partial_j)p\\) and \\(w=z+\\delta e_j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nw\\in\\operatorname{Ab}_q,\n\\qquad\n\\Phi_q^i(w)\\le\\Phi_p^i(z)\\quad\\text{for every }i.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Coordinatewise monotonicity from Lemma 11.6 gives\n\\[\n\\Phi_p^j(z+s)\\le\\Phi_p^j(z)\\le1-1/\\delta&lt;1\n\\]\nfor \\(s\\ge0\\). Therefore\n\\[\nq(z+s)=p(z+s)(1-\\Phi_p^j(z+s))&gt;0,\n\\]\nso \\(z,w\\in\\operatorname{Ab}_q\\).</p><p>Fix \\(i\\), set \\(f(t)=\\Phi_p^i(z+te_j)\\), and commute mixed derivatives of \\(\\log p\\):\n\\[\n\\Phi_q^i(w)=f(\\delta)-\\frac{f'(\\delta)}{1-\\Phi_p^j(w)}.\n\\]\nThe denominator is at least \\(1/\\delta\\), and \\(f'(\\delta)\\le0\\). Convexity gives\n\\[\nf(0)\\ge f(\\delta)-\\delta f'(\\delta).\n\\]\nCombining proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Largest root of the mixed polynomial — paper Lemma A.9.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Largest root of the mixed polynomial — paper Lemma A.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A_i\\succeq0\\), \\(\\sum_iA_i=I_d\\), and \\(\\operatorname{tr}A_i\\le\\varepsilon\\), where \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The largest root of \\(\\mu[A_1,\\ldots,A_m]\\) is at most \\((1+\\sqrt\\varepsilon)^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\np_0(y)=\\det\\left(\\sum_iy_iA_i\\right),\n\\qquad\np_j=(1-\\partial_{y_j})p_{j-1}.\n\\]\nLemma 11.2 gives stability, and\n\\[\n\\mu[A_1,\\ldots,A_m](x)=p_m(x,\\ldots,x),\n\\]\nby substituting \\(y_i=x+z_i\\).</p><p>Choose\n\\[\nt=\\varepsilon+\\sqrt\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n\\delta=1+\\sqrt\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n\\phi=\\varepsilon/t=1-1/\\delta.\n\\]\nAt \\(z^{(0)}=t\\mathbf1\\), the determinant matrix is \\(tI_d\\). Adding nonnegative coordinates adds a positive semidefinite matrix, so \\(z^{(0)}\\in\\operatorname{Ab}_{p_0}\\), and\n\\[\n\\Phi_{p_0}^i(z^{(0)})\n=\\operatorname{tr}((tI_d)^{-1}A_i)\\le\\phi.\n\\]\nInductively set\n\\[\nz^{(j)}=t\\mathbf1+\\delta(e_1+\\cdots+e_j).\n\\]\nLemma 11.7 preserves the above-root property and all barriers at most \\(\\phi\\), because \\(\\phi+1/\\delta=1\\).</p><p>At the end, \\((t+\\delta)\\mathbf1\\) is above the roots of \\(p_m\\). The mixed polynomial is real-rooted by Lemma 11.3 and positive for \\(x\\ge t+\\delta\\); its largest root is at most\n\\[\nt+\\delta=\\varepsilon+2\\sqrt\\varepsilon+1.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Two-sided splitting — paper Lemma 6.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Two-sided splitting — paper Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(w_e\\in\\mathbb R^D\\) are finitely many vectors.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\Sigma=\\sum_ew_ew_e^{\\mathsf T}\\preceq2I_D.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\|w_e\\|^2\\le\\varepsilon\\) for every \\(e\\), where \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\tau=2\\sqrt\\varepsilon+\\varepsilon\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a subset \\(A\\) of the indices such that\n\\[\n\\frac12\\Sigma-\\tau I_D\n\\preceq\n\\sum_{e\\in A}w_ew_e^{\\mathsf T}\n\\preceq\n\\frac12\\Sigma+\\tau I_D.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Independently let \\(v_e\\in\\mathbb R^{2D}\\) equal \\((w_e,0)\\) or \\((0,w_e)\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). Then\n\\[\nM=\\sum_e\\mathbb E[v_ev_e^{\\mathsf T}]\n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\Sigma/2,\\Sigma/2)\\preceq I_{2D},\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathbb E\\|v_e\\|^2\\le\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Diagonalize \\(I_{2D}-M\\). For each positive eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\) with unit eigenvector \\(f\\), set \\(m_\\lambda=\\lceil\\lambda/\\varepsilon\\rceil\\) and introduce \\(m_\\lambda\\) deterministic vectors \\(\\sqrt{\\lambda/m_\\lambda}\\,f\\). Each has squared norm at most \\(\\varepsilon\\), and their outer products sum to \\(I_{2D}-M\\). They are independent singleton-support vectors.</p><p>Proposition 11 applies to the enlarged isotropic family. In a successful outcome, let \\(A\\) be the indices assigned to the first block and \\(S_A=\\sum_{e\\in A}w_ew_e^{\\mathsf T}\\). Subtracting the deterministic padding gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diag}(S_A,\\Sigma-S_A)\n\\preceq\nM+\\bigl((1+\\sqrt\\varepsilon)^2-1\\bigr)I_{2D}\n=M+\\tau I_{2D}.\n\\]\nThe first block gives the upper bound, and the second gives the lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Packing-preserving sparsification — paper Proposition 6.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Packing-preserving sparsification — paper Proposition 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(H\\) has at least two vertices and contains \\(r\\) edge-disjoint spanning trees.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  u=r^{-1/16},\\quad q=\\lfloor(1-u)r\\rfloor,\\quad\n  \\varepsilon=6r^{-1/2},\\quad\n  \\tau=2\\sqrt\\varepsilon+\\varepsilon.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute integer \\(r_{\\mathrm{step}}\\) such that for \\(r\\ge r_{\\mathrm{step}}\\), \\(H\\) has a spanning subgraph \\(H'\\) containing at least\n\\[\nr'=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{(1-4u)r}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\nedge-disjoint spanning trees and satisfying\n\\[\n|\\delta_{H'}(S)|\n\\le\\left(\\frac12+3\\tau\\right)|\\delta_H(S)|\n\\qquad(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V).\n\\]\nThe cutoff may also ensure\n\\[\n\\frac5{16}\\le\\frac{r'}r\\le\\frac12,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{1/2+3\\tau}{r'/r}\\le1+15u.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the cutoff large enough for Proposition 9 and \\(r\\ge16^{16}\\), so \\(u\\le1/16\\). Proposition 9 supplies \\(X\\succ0\\) with\n\\[\nX\\succeq L_H,\\qquad\n\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}X\\mathbf1_S\\le3\\mathbf1_S^{\\mathsf T}L_H\\mathbf1_S,\n\\]\nand \\(q\\) disjoint trees whose edges have \\(R_X(e)\\le4r^{-1/2}\\). Let \\(B\\) be precisely their union.</p><p>Apply Proposition 10 with \\(\\rho=u\\). Set\n\\[\na_e=X^{-1/2}b_e,\\qquad\nw_e=(a_e,z_e),\\qquad\n\\Sigma=\\sum_{e\\in B}w_ew_e^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\]\nThe covariance includes cross blocks, which need not vanish. Nevertheless,\n\\[\n(x,y)^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma(x,y)\n=\n\\sum_{e\\in B}(\\langle x,a_e\\rangle+\\langle y,z_e\\rangle)^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\le\n2x^{\\mathsf T}X^{-1/2}L_BX^{-1/2}x+2\\|y\\|^2\n\\le2(\\|x\\|^2+\\|y\\|^2),\n\\]\nbecause \\(L_B\\preceq L_H\\preceq X\\). Hence \\(0\\preceq\\Sigma\\preceq2I\\), even if \\(\\Sigma\\) is singular.</p><p>Since \\(q\\ge(1-u)r-1\\ge r/2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{1+u}{q}\\le\\frac{17}{8r}\\le2r^{-1/2}\n\\]\nfor \\(r\\ge4\\). Thus \\(\\|w_e\\|^2\\le6r^{-1/2}=\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Proposition 12 selects \\(A\\subseteq E(B)\\). Its two principal-block inequalities give\n\\[\n\\sum_{e\\in A}z_ez_e^{\\mathsf T}\\succeq(1/2-\\tau)I_{n-1},\n\\]\n\\[\nX^{-1/2}L_AX^{-1/2}\n\\preceq\\tfrac12X^{-1/2}L_BX^{-1/2}+\\tau I_n.\n\\]\nCongruence yields\n\\[\nL_A\\preceq\\tfrac12L_B+\\tau X\n\\preceq\\tfrac12L_H+\\tau X.\n\\]\nTesting on \\(\\mathbf1_S\\) proves the cut bound for \\(H'=(V,A)\\).</p><p>In terms of \\(u\\),\n\\[\n\\varepsilon=6u^8,\\qquad\n\\tau=2\\sqrt6\\,u^4+6u^8\\le u/2\n\\quad(0&lt;u\\le1/16).\n\\]\nFor example, after division by \\(u\\), use \\(\\sqrt6&lt;3\\) to bound by\n\\(6(16^{-3}+16^{-7})&lt;1/2\\).</p><p>Proposition 10 gives at least\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{(1/2-\\tau)q}{1+u}\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\ntrees. The rounding margin is\n\\[\n(1-2\\tau)((1-u)r-1)-(1+u)(1-4u)r\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ge\n(1-u)((1-u)r-1)-(1+u)(1-4u)r\n=\nr(u+5u^2)-(1-u)\\ge0,\n\\]\nsince \\(ru=r^{15/16}\\ge1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{(1/2-\\tau)q}{1+u}\\ge\\frac{(1-4u)r}{2},\n\\]\nproving the packing assertion.</p><p>Finally, the floor and \\(1/r\\le u\\) give\n\\[\n\\frac{r'}r\\ge\\frac12-2u-\\frac1r\n\\ge\\frac12-3u\\ge\\frac5{16},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{r'}r\\le\\frac12.\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\tau\\le u/2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{1/2+3\\tau}{r'/r}\n\\le\\frac{1+3u}{1-6u}\n=1+\\frac{9u}{1-6u}\n\\le1+15u.\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 31, "cite"], [1, 32, "cite"], [1, 35, "cite"], [1, 36, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [6, 27, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 1, "cite"], [8, 3, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [10, 27, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [12, 33, "cite"], [13, 27, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 34, "cite"], [14, 35, "cite"], [15, 27, "cite"], [16, 19, "cite"], [16, 24, "cite"], [16, 46, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 24, "cite"], [19, 26, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [22, 26, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [24, 26, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [26, 15, "cite"], [27, 32, "cite"], [28, 31, "cite"], [29, 28, "cite"], [30, 28, "cite"], [31, 47, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 47, "cite"], [35, 34, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [36, 34, "cite"], [37, 46, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [38, 40, "cite"], [38, 41, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [39, 43, "cite"], [39, 45, "cite"], [40, 42, "cite"], [40, 45, "cite"], [41, 42, "cite"], [42, 37, "cite"], [43, 44, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [45, 37, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 0, "cite"]]});
